# user/wsyswl.ad — a Wayland server for the Hamnix desktop.
#
# WHY THIS AND NOT AN X BRIDGE
# ============================
# user/xbridge.ad puts an X application on this desktop by running Xvfb with
# its framebuffer in a file and blitting that file into ONE window. It works,
# and it is a dead end: a framebuffer file holds the COMPOSITED screen, so
# every dialog, popup and second toplevel the application owns arrives fused
# into a single rectangle with no way to separate them. You cannot recover a
# window layout from a picture of a screen.
#
# docs/wayland_passthrough_design.md (in the Hamnix tree) settles the question:
# implement ONE protocol -- a minimal Wayland server -- and you get Wayland
# clients directly AND X11 clients for free through XWayland, which is an X
# server that is itself a Wayland client. A Wayland client does not hand us a
# screen; it hands us one SURFACE per window, with a role, a size and a title.
# One xdg_toplevel becomes one wsys window, which is the thing the bridge could
# never do.
#
# WHAT THE DESIGN CALLS THE GATING RISK IS NOT A RISK HERE
# --------------------------------------------------------
# That document is written against the native kernel, where it concludes
# "Phase 1 is blocked until we add SCM_RIGHTS fd passing": a Wayland client
# gives the compositor its pixels by passing a memfd over the socket in an
# ancillary message, and the native kernel dispatches neither sendmsg nor
# recvmsg. On the Linux line AF_UNIX, SCM_RIGHTS, memfd_create and MAP_SHARED
# are all real and already work. The single hardest thing about this port on
# the other line is, here, a recvmsg call. See user/wsyswl-sys.c, which is
# where that call and its four siblings live until they can move into
# user/linux-syscalls.c.
#
# THE SHAPE
# ---------
#   client (libwayland, in the Debian namespace)
#       |  wire protocol: object id, opcode, args -- over $WAYLAND_DISPLAY
#       |  pixels: a memfd passed with SCM_RIGHTS, mapped MAP_SHARED
#       v
#   wsyswl  -- this program. One wsys window per xdg_toplevel.
#       |  'B' blit + 'D' publish on /dev/wsys/<wid>/draw/ctl
#       |  /dev/wsys/<wid>/keys + /pointer read back for input
#       v
#   wsysd   -- the compositor, which knows nothing about Wayland
#
# The pixel path is the v2 blit protocol, the same one xbridge uses, for the
# same reason: the v1 scene display list can express a widget tree and cannot
# express a photograph. A wl_shm buffer is ARGB8888, which is B,G,R,A in
# memory, which is FMT_BGRA8888 -- so the format conversion is free and the
# only per-pixel work is forcing alpha opaque.
#
# WHAT IS HERE AND WHAT IS NOT
# ----------------------------
# Here: wl_display, wl_registry, wl_compositor, wl_surface, wl_shm +
# wl_shm_pool + wl_buffer, xdg_wm_base + xdg_surface + xdg_toplevel +
# xdg_positioner + xdg_popup, wl_seat + wl_pointer + wl_keyboard with a real
# XKB keymap over a memfd, wl_output + zxdg_output_manager_v1, wp_viewporter,
# and wl_data_device_manager and wl_subcompositor as far as their object
# graphs (GTK will not create a seat without the first; Firefox aborts without
# the second).
#
# The last three exist because Firefox asks for them and stops if they are
# missing; each one is commented where it is implemented. The rule this file
# learned the hard way is that a Wayland client does not report a compositor
# gap, it just goes quiet -- see the frame-callback table and
# send_toplevel_configure for two separate ways that happened.
#
# Not here, and each one is a decision rather than an oversight:
#   * DAMAGE IS IGNORED. Every commit blits the whole surface. Honouring
#     wl_surface.damage would be a straightforward win and the place to start
#     when this is too slow -- and with a browser on the screen it IS slow.
#   * SUBSURFACES ARE FLATTENED into the parent toplevel's window, at the
#     parent's origin -- set_position is recorded and not used. A client that
#     puts its content in a subsurface (Firefox does) draws correctly only
#     because the child covers the parent.
#   * A POPUP GETS ITS OWN undecorated window, placed from its positioner's
#     anchor and gravity. The constraint-adjustment rules (flip/slide/resize
#     when a menu would run off the screen) are not implemented; a popup is
#     clamped onto the screen instead, without telling the client.
#   * ONE OUTPUT, one seat, no clipboard, no drag and drop, no dmabuf. A
#     client that insists on GPU buffers gets nothing; software rendering is
#     the only path.
#   * A RESIZE IS NEVER INITIATED BY US. A client can change its own size and
#     the backbuffer follows it (linux-wsys.c's `geometry` verb calls
#     bb_resize), but wsysd has no way to tell this server that the user
#     dragged a window edge, so a Wayland window cannot be resized by the
#     desktop. A maximised client is therefore told a size up front --
#     MAX_W/MAX_H -- rather than being offered the whole output.
#
# HOW FAR THIS HAS BEEN TESTED: weston-simple-shm, weston-flower and
# weston-terminal each get their own decorated Hamnix window and render, and a
# client that exits gives its backbuffer slot back to the next one. XWayland
# runs against it (`Xwayland -shm -noreset`, software pixman, no GL) and X11
# clients -- xeyes, xclock, xsetroot -- render inside its window. Firefox runs
# as a NATIVE Wayland client with MOZ_ENABLE_WAYLAND=1 and paints its full
# chrome. INPUT has now been driven end to end by writing the /keys and
# /pointer rings directly (see tests/linux/wsys_poke.ad): a client receives
# wl_pointer enter/motion/button and wl_keyboard enter/key.
#
# Usage: wsyswl [<socket-path>]
#   default: $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/wayland-0, or /tmp/wayland-0 if that is unset.
#   Point a client at it with WAYLAND_DISPLAY=<basename> XDG_RUNTIME_DIR=<dir>.

extern def sys_write(fd: int32, buf: Ptr[uint8], count: uint64) -> int64
extern def sys_read(fd: int32, buf: Ptr[uint8], count: uint64) -> int64
extern def sys_read_nb(fd: int32, buf: Ptr[uint8], count: uint64) -> int64
extern def sys_open(path: Ptr[char]) -> int32
extern def sys_open_write(path: Ptr[char]) -> int32
extern def sys_close(fd: int32) -> int32
extern def sys_waitfds(fds: Ptr[int32], nfds: uint64, timeout_ms: int64) -> int64
extern def sys_wsys_free(wid: int32) -> int32
extern def sys_exit(code: uint64)

# user/wsyswl-sys.c — the five entry points the runtime does not have yet.
extern def sys_unix_listen(path: Ptr[char], backlog: int32) -> int32
extern def sys_unix_accept(lfd: int32) -> int32
extern def sys_scm_recv(fd: int32, buf: Ptr[uint8], cap: uint64,
                        fds: Ptr[int32], maxfds: int32,
                        nfds: Ptr[int32]) -> int64
extern def sys_scm_send(fd: int32, buf: Ptr[uint8], n: uint64,
                        passfd: int32) -> int64
extern def sys_mmap_shared(fd: int32, len: uint64) -> uint64
extern def sys_munmap_at(addr: uint64, len: uint64) -> int32
extern def sys_fd_size(fd: int32) -> int64
extern def sys_memfd(name: Ptr[char], len: uint64) -> int32
extern def sys_getenv(name: Ptr[char], buf: Ptr[uint8], cap: uint64) -> int64

# The ONE place a client is allowed to ask how big the screen is. See
# lib/hamscreen.ad: every DE client used to answer this question privately and
# every one of them answered it wrong. This server answers it for EVERY
# Wayland client at once -- and, through Xwayland, for every X11 client -- so
# getting it wrong here is that bug multiplied by the whole distribution.
from lib.hamscreen import hamscreen_dims_or_fail


# --- protocol constants ---------------------------------------------

MAXCONN: uint64 = 4
MAXOBJ:  uint64 = 1024                 # client ids stay dense and low --
                                       # but Xwayland is a bigger client
                                       # than anything this server carried
                                       # before, and an id at or above
                                       # MAXOBJ is an object that is
                                       # UNKNOWN FOR EVER: every later
                                       # request to it falls off the end of
                                       # dispatch in silence. It is counted
                                       # now (obj_id_refused), and the
                                       # ceiling is four times higher.
ACC:     uint64 = 8192                 # per-connection reassembly buffer

T_NONE: int32 = 0
T_DISPLAY: int32 = 1
T_REGISTRY: int32 = 2
T_CALLBACK: int32 = 3
T_COMPOSITOR: int32 = 4
T_SHM: int32 = 5
T_SHM_POOL: int32 = 6
T_BUFFER: int32 = 7
T_SURFACE: int32 = 8
T_REGION: int32 = 9
T_XDG_WM: int32 = 10
T_XDG_SURF: int32 = 11
T_XDG_TOP: int32 = 12
T_SEAT: int32 = 13
T_POINTER: int32 = 14
T_KEYBOARD: int32 = 15
T_OUTPUT: int32 = 16
T_DDM: int32 = 17
T_DATA_DEVICE: int32 = 18
T_DATA_SOURCE: int32 = 19
T_SUBCOMPOSITOR: int32 = 20
T_SUBSURFACE: int32 = 21
T_VIEWPORTER: int32 = 22
T_VIEWPORT: int32 = 23
T_XDG_OUT_MGR: int32 = 24
T_XDG_OUT: int32 = 25
T_POSITIONER: int32 = 26
T_XDG_POPUP: int32 = 27

G_COMPOSITOR: uint32 = 1
G_SHM: uint32 = 2
G_XDG: uint32 = 3
G_SEAT: uint32 = 4
G_OUTPUT: uint32 = 5
G_DDM: uint32 = 6
G_SUBCOMPOSITOR: uint32 = 7
G_VIEWPORTER: uint32 = 8
G_XDG_OUTPUT: uint32 = 9

DISPLAY_ID: uint32 = 1

SEAT_VERSION: uint32 = 5
OUTPUT_VERSION: uint32 = 3
SEAT_CAP_POINTER: uint32 = 1
SEAT_CAP_KEYBOARD: uint32 = 2

SHM_ARGB8888: uint32 = 0
SHM_XRGB8888: uint32 = 1

STATE_RELEASED: uint32 = 0
STATE_PRESSED: uint32 = 1
TOP_STATE_MAXIMIZED: uint32 = 1
TOP_STATE_ACTIVATED: uint32 = 4

# What a maximised toplevel is told it may have. wsysd draws a title bar and a
# frame around every window, so "maximised" here cannot mean the whole output:
# a client handed the whole screen would have its chrome pushed off the bottom
# by the decoration it cannot see. Set from the real screen size at startup --
# these are the inset, not a resolution.
MAX_INSET_W: int32 = 120
MAX_INSET_H: int32 = 100
MAX_W: int32 = 1160
MAX_H: int32 = 700

BTN_LEFT: uint32 = 272
BTN_MIDDLE: uint32 = 274
BTN_RIGHT: uint32 = 273

MOD_SHIFT: uint32 = 1

# The blit protocol's FMT_BGRA8888. wl_shm ARGB8888 is byte-for-byte this.
FMT_BGRA8888: uint8 = 2


# --- state ------------------------------------------------------------

listen_fd: int32 = -1
serial: uint32 = 0
# NOT a default and not a guess -- resolve_screen() overwrites both from
# /dev/wsys/screen before the socket exists, and refuses to serve if the
# compositor has not published one.
scr_w: int32 = 0
scr_h: int32 = 0

conn_inuse: Array[4, int32]
conn_fd:    Array[4, int32]
conn_reg:   Array[4, int32]
conn_acc:   Array[32768, uint8]        # 4 * 8192
conn_acclen: Array[4, uint64]
conn_seat:  Array[4, int32]
conn_pointer: Array[4, int32]
conn_keyboard: Array[4, int32]
conn_xdg_wm: Array[4, int32]
conn_output: Array[4, int32]           # the client's wl_output object, for enter

TITLE_CAP: uint64 = 48
conn_title: Array[192, uint8]          # 4 * 48
conn_title_len: Array[4, uint64]

# Per object, indexed conn * MAXOBJ + id.
obj_type: Array[4096, int32]
obj_a: Array[4096, int32]
obj_b: Array[4096, int32]
obj_c: Array[4096, int32]
obj_d: Array[4096, int32]
obj_e: Array[4096, int32]
obj_f: Array[4096, int32]
obj_g: Array[4096, int32]
# Two more per-object words, added for xdg_positioner: placing a menu needs a
# size, an anchor rect, an anchor, a gravity and an offset, which is ten
# numbers, and obj_a..obj_g are seven.
obj_h: Array[4096, int32]
obj_i: Array[4096, int32]
surf_parent: Array[4096, int32]

# SHM MAPPINGS ARE OWNED BY THE CONNECTION, NOT BY THE POOL OBJECT.
#
# A client is entitled to destroy a wl_shm_pool the instant it has carved its
# wl_buffers out of it -- "the mapping will be released when all the buffers
# have been destroyed" -- and weston-simple-shm does exactly that, three
# requests after create_pool and long before it attaches anything. Unmapping
# on pool destroy therefore threw away the pixels of every buffer the client
# was about to draw with, and the first commit found nothing to blit. So a
# mapping lives in this per-connection table, both the pool and its buffers
# hold an INDEX into it, and it is unmapped when the client goes.
#
# ...AND IT IS REFERENCE COUNTED, or a browser runs out of them. The pool
# holds one reference and every wl_buffer cut from it holds another; the
# mapping is unmapped when the last of them goes. Waiting for the connection
# to close instead is fine for a client with one pool and fatal for Firefox,
# which creates a fresh pool for every new buffer size and destroys the old
# one immediately: the slots filled up, map_alloc started returning -1, and
# from that moment every commit was dropped. What that looked like was a
# browser whose window painted and whose MENUS were invisible -- the menu was
# simply the first surface unlucky enough to be created after the table was
# full.
#
# SIXTEEN WAS THE STEAM BUG, MEASURED IN BOTH DIRECTIONS. Steam's X session
# holds TWENTY-SIX of these at once. With the table at 16 it fills, map_alloc
# returns -1, and from that moment every commit is dropped at `mi < 0` -- so
# Xwayland's ONE rootful surface freezes and both the login window and the
# xterm sitting behind it stop reaching the framebuffer, which is exactly what
# three passes of this investigation were looking at. Two boots of the same
# image differing only in this number: at 16, `map_alloc_failed 10`,
# `drop_no_mapping 508`, 3 commits, a black screen and an xterm still at the
# size it started at; at 64, `maps_high_water 26`, zero drops, 506 commits, and
# `Sign in to Steam` on the desktop.
#
# A bare Xwayland with one xterm on it already touches 8, so 16 was half a
# table for the smallest session there is. Sixty-four costs 2 KB of BSS.
MAXMAP: uint64 = 64
map_addr: Array[256, uint64]           # 4 conns * 64
map_len:  Array[256, uint64]
map_used: Array[256, int32]
map_ref:  Array[256, int32]

# RECEIVED DESCRIPTORS ARE A QUEUE, NOT A PROPERTY OF THE MESSAGE.
#
# The kernel hands ancillary data to the recvmsg that read the FIRST BYTE of
# the sendmsg it accompanied -- not to the message inside that stream which
# actually wants it. A client flushes create_pool together with create_buffer
# and destroy, so pairing "the fd from this read" with "the first request in
# this read" attached the memfd to whatever happened to be at the front, and
# create_pool saw nothing. libwayland's own connection code does the only
# thing that works: push received fds onto a FIFO and let each request with an
# fd argument pop one, in order. This is that FIFO.
FDQ: uint64 = 8
fdq:     Array[32, int32]              # 4 conns * 8
fdq_head: Array[4, uint64]
fdq_tail: Array[4, uint64]

# FRAME CALLBACKS ARE A LIST PER SURFACE, NOT ONE SLOT.
#
# wl_surface.frame may be called any number of times before the commit that
# arms them; the spec says the callbacks are queued and all of them fire on the
# next frame. Keeping only the newest looks harmless and is not: Firefox asks
# for its INITIAL-DRAW callback on the toplevel surface while it is still
# building the window, commits only the subsurface for a while, and asks for a
# second one on the toplevel when it finally attaches a buffer. With one slot
# the first request is overwritten and never answered -- and that first one is
# the one Firefox waits on to decide the window is ready to draw. The window
# comes up, renders one empty frame, and stops forever, with nothing in any log
# to say why. This table is the fix.
FCMAX: uint64 = 64
fc_conn: Array[64, int32]              # -1 when the slot is free
fc_surf: Array[64, int32]
fc_cb:   Array[64, int32]

out:     Array[8192, uint8]
out_pos: uint64 = 0
out_fd:  int32 = -1                    # fd to pass with the next flush, or -1

rowbuf:  Array[8192, uint8]
blithdr: Array[32, uint8]
pathbuf: Array[256, uint8]
ctlbuf:  Array[256, uint8]
rbuf:    Array[256, uint8]
numbuf:  Array[24, uint8]
waitset: Array[16, int32]
recvfds: Array[8, int32]
recvn:   Array[2, int32]
sockpath: Array[256, uint8]
envbuf:  Array[256, uint8]

# wid -> the fds we hold open on that window. Small and linear: a session has
# a handful of windows and the lookup is off the hot path.
MAXWIN: uint64 = 12
w_wid:  Array[12, int32]
w_draw: Array[12, int32]
w_keys: Array[12, int32]
w_ptr:  Array[12, int32]
w_conn: Array[12, int32]
w_surf: Array[12, int32]
w_btn:  Array[12, int32]               # last pointer button mask seen
w_in:   Array[12, int32]               # pointer has entered this surface
w_focus: Array[12, int32]              # keyboard focus is on this surface
w_x:    Array[12, int32]               # where this window was placed, so a
w_y:    Array[12, int32]               # popup can be placed against it
n_win:  uint64 = 0
cascade: int32 = 0

# One line of a /keys or /pointer ring, reassembled across reads.
inbuf:  Array[1024, uint8]
carry:  Array[1024, uint8]             # 12 windows would need 12 of these; the
carrylen: uint64 = 0                   # rings are line-atomic in practice, so
                                       # one shared carry is honest enough --
                                       # see drain_window.

keymap_fd: int32 = -1
keymap_len: uint64 = 0
kmbuf: Array[8192, uint8]
kmlen: uint64 = 0

evt_time: uint32 = 0
verbose: int32 = 0
tick: int32 = 0


# --- small helpers ----------------------------------------------------

def slen(s: Ptr[uint8]) -> uint64:
    n: uint64 = 0
    while s[n] != 0:
        n = n + 1
    return n


def puts(fd: int32, s: Ptr[uint8]):
    sys_write(fd, s, slen(s))


def putd(fd: int32, v: int32):
    n: uint64 = 0
    x: int32 = v
    if x < 0:
        sys_write(fd, cast[Ptr[uint8]]("-"), 1)
        x = 0 - x
    if x == 0:
        numbuf[0] = 48
        n = 1
    while x > 0:
        numbuf[n] = cast[uint8](48 + x % 10)
        x = x / 10
        n = n + 1
    i: uint64 = n
    while i > 0:
        i = i - 1
        sys_write(fd, &numbuf[i], 1)


def emit_int(buf: Ptr[uint8], at: uint64, v: int32) -> uint64:
    d: Array[12, uint8]
    n: uint64 = 0
    x: int32 = v
    i: uint64 = at
    if x < 0:
        buf[i] = 45
        i = i + 1
        x = 0 - x
    if x == 0:
        d[0] = 48
        n = 1
    while x > 0:
        d[n] = cast[uint8](48 + x % 10)
        x = x / 10
        n = n + 1
    while n > 0:
        n = n - 1
        buf[i] = d[n]
        i = i + 1
    return i


def put_le32(buf: Ptr[uint8], at: uint64, v: int32):
    u: uint32 = cast[uint32](v)
    buf[at]     = cast[uint8](u & 255)
    buf[at + 1] = cast[uint8]((u >> 8) & 255)
    buf[at + 2] = cast[uint8]((u >> 16) & 255)
    buf[at + 3] = cast[uint8]((u >> 24) & 255)


def ru32(p: Ptr[uint8], off: uint64) -> uint32:
    return cast[uint32](p[off]) \
         | (cast[uint32](p[off + 1]) << 8) \
         | (cast[uint32](p[off + 2]) << 16) \
         | (cast[uint32](p[off + 3]) << 24)


def str_copy(dst: Ptr[uint8], s: Ptr[uint8], cap: uint64) -> uint64:
    i: uint64 = 0
    while s[i] != 0 and i + 1 < cap:
        dst[i] = s[i]
        i = i + 1
    dst[i] = 0
    return i


# --- counters: a dropped frame must never be silent -------------------
#
# commit_buffer used to have SIX bare `return`s in it, and every one of them
# means "this client's pixels are not going on the screen this frame". A
# compositor that does that without saying so is the exact failure this tree is
# organised against: it cost three passes to work out that a Steam window which
# X says is mapped, viewable and PAINTED never reached the framebuffer, because
# the one process that knew it had thrown the buffer away said nothing.
#
# WHY COUNTERS AND NOT A LOG. WSYSWL_VERBOSE=1 prints a line per commit into
# /var/log on the initramfs tmpfs -- the VM's RAM -- and the guest went silent
# inside two minutes of Steam starting, twice. So: a monotonic counter per
# reason, ONE named line on stderr the first time each fires, and the whole set
# published as a small file beside the socket, which is the one directory that
# spans the namespace boundary (§6 of docs/steam_namespace.md). Reading a
# server's own state with `cat` is how the wsysd bug before this one was
# caught; `/dev/wsys/wsysd/state` is the precedent.
NSTAT: uint64 = 20
stat_n:    Array[20, int32]
stat_said: Array[20, int32]
stat_dirty: int32 = 1

S_COMMIT:   uint64 = 0          # buffers actually blitted to a window
S_ROWS:     uint64 = 1          # rows blitted
S_NOTBUF:   uint64 = 2          # attached id is not a wl_buffer
S_BADDIM:   uint64 = 3          # width or height < 1
S_NOMAP:    uint64 = 4          # mi < 0: the buffer has no shm mapping
S_NOBASE:   uint64 = 5          # the mapping is not mapped
S_NOROLE:   uint64 = 6          # a surface with no xdg_surface (cursor)
S_NOWIN:    uint64 = 7          # win_open failed
S_NOSLOT:   uint64 = 8          # the window lost its wsysd slot
S_UNCONF:   uint64 = 9          # commit with a buffer before ack_configure
S_SHORT:    uint64 = 10         # pos + rowbytes > maplen: rows cut off
S_MAPFULL:  uint64 = 11         # map_alloc found no free slot
S_IDHIGH:   uint64 = 12         # an object id at or above MAXOBJ
S_FCFULL:   uint64 = 13         # the frame-callback table was full
S_MAPUSE:   uint64 = 14         # gauge: mappings in use right now
S_MAPHI:    uint64 = 15         # high water of the above
S_IDHI:     uint64 = 16         # highest object id ever seen
S_WINHI:    uint64 = 17         # high water of open windows

statepath: Array[256, uint8]


def hit(k: uint64):
    stat_n[k] = stat_n[k] + 1
    stat_dirty = 1


def gauge(k: uint64, v: int32):
    if stat_n[k] != v:
        stat_n[k] = v
        stat_dirty = 1


def high(k: uint64, v: int32):
    if v > stat_n[k]:
        stat_n[k] = v
        stat_dirty = 1


# A drop, counted, and NAMED ONCE on stderr. Once, because the thing that made
# the last attempt at this useless was volume.
def drop(k: uint64, name: Ptr[uint8]):
    stat_n[k] = stat_n[k] + 1
    stat_dirty = 1
    if stat_said[k] == 0:
        stat_said[k] = 1
        puts(2, "wsyswl: DROPPING FRAMES -- ")
        puts(2, name)
        puts(2, " (first time; running totals in ")
        puts(2, &statepath[0])
        puts(2, ")\n")


def st_kv(fd: int32, name: Ptr[uint8], k: uint64):
    puts(fd, name)
    puts(fd, " ")
    putd(fd, stat_n[k])
    puts(fd, "\n")


# --- wire output ------------------------------------------------------

def o_u32(v: uint32):
    if out_pos + 4 > 8192:
        return
    put_le32(&out[0], out_pos, cast[int32](v))
    out_pos = out_pos + 4


def o_cstr(s: Ptr[uint8]):
    # A Wayland string arg: u32 length INCLUDING the NUL, the bytes, the NUL,
    # then zero padding to a 32-bit boundary.
    n: uint64 = slen(s)
    o_u32(cast[uint32](n + 1))
    i: uint64 = 0
    while i < n and out_pos < 8192:
        out[out_pos] = s[i]
        out_pos = out_pos + 1
        i = i + 1
    out[out_pos] = 0
    out_pos = out_pos + 1
    while (out_pos & 3) != 0:
        out[out_pos] = 0
        out_pos = out_pos + 1


def emit_begin(obj: uint32) -> uint64:
    start: uint64 = out_pos
    o_u32(obj)
    o_u32(0)                                    # size<<16 | opcode, patched
    return start


def emit_end(start: uint64, opcode: uint32):
    size: uint64 = out_pos - start
    put_le32(&out[0], start + 4,
             cast[int32]((cast[uint32](size) << 16) | (opcode & 0xffff)))


# Everything staged since the last flush goes out in ONE sendmsg. Wayland is a
# stream and a client parses it incrementally, so a short write would leave
# half an event on the wire and desynchronise its parser for good; sys_scm_send
# blocks until the whole burst is gone, which is the only safe choice.
def flush(fd: int32):
    if out_pos == 0 and out_fd < 0:
        return
    sys_scm_send(fd, &out[0], out_pos, out_fd)
    if out_fd >= 0:
        sys_close(out_fd)
        out_fd = -1
    out_pos = 0


# --- object table -----------------------------------------------------

def oidx(c: uint64, id: uint32) -> uint64:
    return c * MAXOBJ + cast[uint64](id)


def valid_id(id: uint32) -> int32:
    if id == 0:
        return 0
    if cast[uint64](id) >= MAXOBJ:
        return 0
    return 1


def new_obj(c: uint64, id: uint32, otype: int32):
    if valid_id(id) == 0:
        # AN ID THIS SERVER REFUSES IS AN OBJECT THAT IS UNKNOWN FOR EVER.
        # Every later request addressed to it falls off the end of dispatch and
        # is consumed in silence -- so if it was a wl_buffer, that client's
        # pixels stop arriving and nothing anywhere says why.
        if id != 0:
            drop(S_IDHIGH, "an object id at or above MAXOBJ -- raise MAXOBJ")
        return
    high(S_IDHI, cast[int32](id))
    oi: uint64 = oidx(c, id)
    obj_type[oi] = otype
    obj_a[oi] = 0
    obj_b[oi] = 0
    obj_c[oi] = 0
    obj_d[oi] = 0
    obj_e[oi] = 0
    obj_f[oi] = 0
    obj_g[oi] = -1
    obj_h[oi] = 0
    obj_i[oi] = 0
    surf_parent[oi] = 0


def fdq_push(c: uint64, fd: int32):
    if fdq_tail[c] - fdq_head[c] >= FDQ:
        sys_close(fd)                       # a client sending fds we never
        return                              # consume is a client bug, not a leak
    fdq[c * FDQ + fdq_tail[c] % FDQ] = fd
    fdq_tail[c] = fdq_tail[c] + 1


def fdq_pop(c: uint64) -> int32:
    if fdq_head[c] == fdq_tail[c]:
        return -1
    fd: int32 = fdq[c * FDQ + fdq_head[c] % FDQ]
    fdq_head[c] = fdq_head[c] + 1
    return fd


def fc_add(c: uint64, surf: uint32, cb: uint32) -> int32:
    i: uint64 = 0
    while i < FCMAX:
        if fc_conn[i] < 0:
            fc_conn[i] = cast[int32](c)
            fc_surf[i] = cast[int32](surf)
            fc_cb[i] = cast[int32](cb)
            return 1
        i = i + 1
    return 0


def fc_drop_surf(c: uint64, surf: uint32):
    i: uint64 = 0
    while i < FCMAX:
        if fc_conn[i] == cast[int32](c) and fc_surf[i] == cast[int32](surf):
            fc_conn[i] = -1
        i = i + 1


def fc_drop_conn(c: uint64):
    i: uint64 = 0
    while i < FCMAX:
        if fc_conn[i] == cast[int32](c):
            fc_conn[i] = -1
        i = i + 1


def map_count(c: uint64) -> int32:
    n: int32 = 0
    i: uint64 = 0
    while i < MAXMAP:
        if map_used[c * MAXMAP + i] != 0:
            n = n + 1
        i = i + 1
    return n


def map_alloc(c: uint64) -> int32:
    i: uint64 = 0
    while i < MAXMAP:
        if map_used[c * MAXMAP + i] == 0:
            map_used[c * MAXMAP + i] = 1
            map_addr[c * MAXMAP + i] = 0
            map_len[c * MAXMAP + i] = 0
            map_ref[c * MAXMAP + i] = 1         # the pool's own reference
            n: int32 = map_count(c)
            gauge(S_MAPUSE, n)
            high(S_MAPHI, n)
            return cast[int32](i)
        i = i + 1
    drop(S_MAPFULL, "wl_shm mappings exhausted -- raise MAXMAP")
    return -1


def map_ref_up(c: uint64, mi: int32):
    if mi < 0:
        return
    m: uint64 = c * MAXMAP + cast[uint64](mi)
    if map_used[m] != 0:
        map_ref[m] = map_ref[m] + 1


def map_unref(c: uint64, mi: int32):
    if mi < 0:
        return
    m: uint64 = c * MAXMAP + cast[uint64](mi)
    if map_used[m] == 0:
        return
    map_ref[m] = map_ref[m] - 1
    if map_ref[m] > 0:
        return
    if map_addr[m] != 0:
        sys_munmap_at(map_addr[m], map_len[m])
    map_addr[m] = 0
    map_len[m] = 0
    map_used[m] = 0
    gauge(S_MAPUSE, map_count(c))


def obj_get_type(c: uint64, id: uint32) -> int32:
    if valid_id(id) == 0:
        return T_NONE
    return obj_type[oidx(c, id)]


def free_obj(c: uint64, id: uint32):
    if valid_id(id) == 0:
        return
    obj_type[oidx(c, id)] = T_NONE


# --- events -----------------------------------------------------------

def send_delete_id(id: uint32):
    s: uint64 = emit_begin(DISPLAY_ID)
    o_u32(id)
    emit_end(s, 1)


def send_callback_done(cb: uint32, data: uint32):
    s: uint64 = emit_begin(cb)
    o_u32(data)
    emit_end(s, 0)


def send_global(reg: uint32, name: uint32, iface: Ptr[uint8], version: uint32):
    s: uint64 = emit_begin(reg)
    o_u32(name)
    o_cstr(iface)
    o_u32(version)
    emit_end(s, 0)


def send_shm_format(shm: uint32, fmt: uint32):
    s: uint64 = emit_begin(shm)
    o_u32(fmt)
    emit_end(s, 0)


# wl_surface.enter(output) -- "this surface is now on that monitor".
#
# It reads like a hint and it is not. A toolkit learns the scale, the refresh
# and the colour of a surface from the output it is ON, and until it has been
# told it is on one it has no output at all: GTK's wl_surface has an empty
# output list, so Firefox's compositor thread has nothing to schedule frames
# against and never produces a second frame. Weston sends this the moment a
# surface is mapped; not sending it is why Firefox drew one empty window and
# then sat still.
def send_surface_enter(surface: uint32, output: uint32):
    s: uint64 = emit_begin(surface)
    o_u32(output)
    emit_end(s, 0)


def send_buffer_release(buf: uint32):
    s: uint64 = emit_begin(buf)
    emit_end(s, 0)


def send_xdg_configure(xs: uint32, ser: uint32):
    s: uint64 = emit_begin(xs)
    o_u32(ser)
    emit_end(s, 0)


def send_toplevel_configure(top: uint32, w: uint32, h: uint32, maxed: int32):
    # (0,0) means "you choose your own size", which is what the initial
    # configure must say for a client that has not asked for anything. ACTIVATED
    # goes on every configure so a client draws its focused decorations -- there
    # is one window manager here and it is wsysd, so a Wayland client is never
    # told it is inactive.
    #
    # MAXIMIZED IS NOT COSMETIC. A client that sent set_maximized has made a
    # request the compositor is required to answer, and it answers with the
    # state in a configure -- there is no other channel. Firefox maximises its
    # first window, and until the configure comes back carrying MAXIMIZED *and*
    # a concrete size it renders exactly one frame (an empty client-side-
    # decorated frame with a shadow) and then stops dead, waiting for a size it
    # was promised. That looks identical to a client that crashed on startup.
    s: uint64 = emit_begin(top)
    o_u32(w)
    o_u32(h)
    if maxed != 0:
        o_u32(8)                                # array: two u32 states
        o_u32(TOP_STATE_MAXIMIZED)
        o_u32(TOP_STATE_ACTIVATED)
    else:
        o_u32(4)
        o_u32(TOP_STATE_ACTIVATED)
    emit_end(s, 0)


def send_toplevel_close(top: uint32):
    s: uint64 = emit_begin(top)
    emit_end(s, 1)


def send_seat_caps(seat: uint32, caps: uint32):
    s: uint64 = emit_begin(seat)
    o_u32(caps)
    emit_end(s, 0)


def send_seat_name(seat: uint32, name: Ptr[uint8]):
    s: uint64 = emit_begin(seat)
    o_cstr(name)
    emit_end(s, 1)


def send_output_all(output: uint32):
    # geometry(x, y, phys_w, phys_h, subpixel, make, model, transform)
    s: uint64 = emit_begin(output)
    o_u32(0)
    o_u32(0)
    o_u32(cast[uint32](scr_w))
    o_u32(cast[uint32](scr_h))
    o_u32(0)
    o_cstr("Hamnix")
    o_cstr("wsys")
    o_u32(0)
    emit_end(s, 0)
    # mode(flags = current|preferred, w, h, refresh mHz)
    s = emit_begin(output)
    o_u32(3)
    o_u32(cast[uint32](scr_w))
    o_u32(cast[uint32](scr_h))
    o_u32(60000)
    emit_end(s, 1)
    # scale(1)
    s = emit_begin(output)
    o_u32(1)
    emit_end(s, 3)
    # done()
    s = emit_begin(output)
    emit_end(s, 2)


# zxdg_output_v1: where the monitor is, in the coordinate space a client
# actually thinks in.
#
# wl_output.geometry reports PHYSICAL millimetres and wl_output.mode reports
# the mode in device pixels; neither answers "how big is this screen in the
# units my windows are measured in", which is what a toolkit enumerating
# monitors wants. xdg-output does, and Firefox binds it before it lays
# anything out. A Firefox that cannot enumerate a single monitor brings its
# window up and then has nowhere to render it.
def send_xdg_output_all(xo: uint32):
    s: uint64 = emit_begin(xo)                  # logical_position
    o_u32(0)
    o_u32(0)
    emit_end(s, 0)
    s = emit_begin(xo)                          # logical_size
    o_u32(cast[uint32](scr_w))
    o_u32(cast[uint32](scr_h))
    emit_end(s, 1)
    s = emit_begin(xo)                          # name (v2)
    o_cstr("HAM-0")
    emit_end(s, 3)
    s = emit_begin(xo)                          # description (v2)
    o_cstr("Hamnix wsys display")
    emit_end(s, 4)
    s = emit_begin(xo)                          # done
    emit_end(s, 2)


def fixed(v: int32) -> uint32:
    # wl_fixed_t is signed 24.8.
    return cast[uint32](v << 8)


def send_kbd_keymap(kbd: uint32, size: uint32):
    s: uint64 = emit_begin(kbd)
    o_u32(1)                                    # XKB_V1
    o_u32(size)
    emit_end(s, 0)


def send_kbd_enter(kbd: uint32, ser: uint32, surface: uint32):
    s: uint64 = emit_begin(kbd)
    o_u32(ser)
    o_u32(surface)
    o_u32(0)                                    # keys[] : empty array
    emit_end(s, 1)


def send_kbd_leave(kbd: uint32, ser: uint32, surface: uint32):
    s: uint64 = emit_begin(kbd)
    o_u32(ser)
    o_u32(surface)
    emit_end(s, 2)


def send_kbd_key(kbd: uint32, ser: uint32, t: uint32, key: uint32,
                 state: uint32):
    s: uint64 = emit_begin(kbd)
    o_u32(ser)
    o_u32(t)
    o_u32(key)
    o_u32(state)
    emit_end(s, 3)


def send_kbd_modifiers(kbd: uint32, ser: uint32, dep: uint32):
    s: uint64 = emit_begin(kbd)
    o_u32(ser)
    o_u32(dep)
    o_u32(0)
    o_u32(0)
    o_u32(0)
    emit_end(s, 4)


def send_kbd_repeat_info(kbd: uint32, rate: uint32, delay: uint32):
    s: uint64 = emit_begin(kbd)
    o_u32(rate)
    o_u32(delay)
    emit_end(s, 5)


def send_ptr_enter(ptr: uint32, ser: uint32, surface: uint32,
                   sx: int32, sy: int32):
    s: uint64 = emit_begin(ptr)
    o_u32(ser)
    o_u32(surface)
    o_u32(fixed(sx))
    o_u32(fixed(sy))
    emit_end(s, 0)


def send_ptr_leave(ptr: uint32, ser: uint32, surface: uint32):
    s: uint64 = emit_begin(ptr)
    o_u32(ser)
    o_u32(surface)
    emit_end(s, 1)


def send_ptr_motion(ptr: uint32, t: uint32, sx: int32, sy: int32):
    s: uint64 = emit_begin(ptr)
    o_u32(t)
    o_u32(fixed(sx))
    o_u32(fixed(sy))
    emit_end(s, 2)


def send_ptr_button(ptr: uint32, ser: uint32, t: uint32, btn: uint32,
                    state: uint32):
    s: uint64 = emit_begin(ptr)
    o_u32(ser)
    o_u32(t)
    o_u32(btn)
    o_u32(state)
    emit_end(s, 3)


def send_ptr_frame(ptr: uint32):
    s: uint64 = emit_begin(ptr)
    emit_end(s, 5)


# --- the XKB keymap ---------------------------------------------------
#
# wl_keyboard.keymap hands the client an fd holding an XKB keymap, and the
# client compiles it with libxkbcommon before it will interpret a single key.
# This one is SELF-CONTAINED -- it includes no XKB data files, because the
# client resolves includes on ITS side and the Debian namespace's xkb data may
# not be where a client expects. It covers the ASCII the Hamnix keyboard
# produces, with a Shift level so uppercase resolves. It is the reference
# implementation's keymap (Hamnix linux_abi/wayland.ad), transcribed.

def km(s: Ptr[uint8]):
    i: uint64 = 0
    while s[i] != 0 and kmlen < 8190:
        kmbuf[kmlen] = s[i]
        kmlen = kmlen + 1
        i = i + 1


def km_key(name: Ptr[uint8], syms: Ptr[uint8]):
    km("key <")
    km(name)
    km("> {type[Group1]=\"TWO_LEVEL\",[")
    km(syms)
    km("]};\n")


def build_keymap():
    if keymap_fd >= 0:
        return
    kmlen = 0
    km("xkb_keymap {\n")
    km("xkb_keycodes \"hm\" {\n")
    km("minimum = 8;\nmaximum = 255;\n")
    # A keycode is the evdev code plus 8 -- the client adds 8 to whatever the
    # server sends in wl_keyboard.key, so the names below must land on that.
    km("<A9> = 23; <A22> = 22; <A28> = 36; <A57> = 65;\n")
    km("<K02>=10;<K03>=11;<K04>=12;<K05>=13;<K06>=14;\n")
    km("<K07>=15;<K08>=16;<K09>=17;<K10>=18;<K11>=19;\n")
    km("<K12>=20;<K13>=21;<K16>=24;<K17>=25;\n")
    km("<K18>=26;<K19>=27;<K20>=28;<K21>=29;<K22>=30;\n")
    km("<K23>=31;<K24>=32;<K25>=33;<K26>=34;<K27>=35;\n")
    km("<K30>=38;<K31>=39;<K32>=40;<K33>=41;<K34>=42;\n")
    km("<K35>=43;<K36>=44;<K37>=45;<K38>=46;<K39>=47;\n")
    km("<K40>=48;<K41>=49;<K42>=50;<K43>=51;<K44>=52;\n")
    km("<K45>=53;<K46>=54;<K47>=55;<K48>=56;<K49>=57;\n")
    km("<K50>=58;<K51>=59;<K52>=60;<K53>=61;\n")
    km("<LFSH>=50;<RTSH>=62;\n")
    km("};\n")
    km("xkb_types \"hm\" {\n")
    km("virtual_modifiers NumLock;\n")
    km("type \"ONE_LEVEL\" { modifiers=none; level_name[Level1]=\"Any\"; };\n")
    km("type \"TWO_LEVEL\" { modifiers=Shift; map[Shift]=Level2; ")
    km("level_name[Level1]=\"Base\"; level_name[Level2]=\"Shift\"; };\n")
    km("};\n")
    km("xkb_compatibility \"hm\" { };\n")
    km("xkb_symbols \"hm\" {\n")
    km("key <LFSH> {[Shift_L]};\nkey <RTSH> {[Shift_R]};\n")
    km("key <A9> {[Tab]};\nkey <A22> {[BackSpace]};\n")
    km("key <A28> {[Return]};\nkey <A57> {[space]};\n")
    km_key("K02", "1,exclam")
    km_key("K03", "2,at")
    km_key("K04", "3,numbersign")
    km_key("K05", "4,dollar")
    km_key("K06", "5,percent")
    km_key("K07", "6,asciicircum")
    km_key("K08", "7,ampersand")
    km_key("K09", "8,asterisk")
    km_key("K10", "9,parenleft")
    km_key("K11", "0,parenright")
    km_key("K12", "minus,underscore")
    km_key("K13", "equal,plus")
    km_key("K16", "q,Q")
    km_key("K17", "w,W")
    km_key("K18", "e,E")
    km_key("K19", "r,R")
    km_key("K20", "t,T")
    km_key("K21", "y,Y")
    km_key("K22", "u,U")
    km_key("K23", "i,I")
    km_key("K24", "o,O")
    km_key("K25", "p,P")
    km_key("K26", "bracketleft,braceleft")
    km_key("K27", "bracketright,braceright")
    km_key("K30", "a,A")
    km_key("K31", "s,S")
    km_key("K32", "d,D")
    km_key("K33", "f,F")
    km_key("K34", "g,G")
    km_key("K35", "h,H")
    km_key("K36", "j,J")
    km_key("K37", "k,K")
    km_key("K38", "l,L")
    km_key("K39", "semicolon,colon")
    km_key("K40", "apostrophe,quotedbl")
    km_key("K41", "grave,asciitilde")
    km_key("K43", "backslash,bar")
    km_key("K44", "z,Z")
    km_key("K45", "x,X")
    km_key("K46", "c,C")
    km_key("K47", "v,V")
    km_key("K48", "b,B")
    km_key("K49", "n,N")
    km_key("K50", "m,M")
    km_key("K51", "comma,less")
    km_key("K52", "period,greater")
    km_key("K53", "slash,question")
    km("modifier_map Shift {<LFSH>,<RTSH>};\n")
    km("};\n")
    km("};\n")
    kmbuf[kmlen] = 0
    keymap_len = kmlen + 1                      # the client mmaps the NUL too
    fd: int32 = sys_memfd(cast[Ptr[char]]("wl-xkb-keymap"), keymap_len)
    if fd < 0:
        return
    sys_write(fd, &kmbuf[0], keymap_len)
    keymap_fd = fd


# --- windows ----------------------------------------------------------

def winpath(wid: int32, leaf: Ptr[uint8]):
    i: uint64 = 0
    p: Ptr[uint8] = "/dev/wsys/"
    k: uint64 = 0
    while p[k] != 0:
        pathbuf[i] = p[k]
        i = i + 1
        k = k + 1
    i = emit_int(&pathbuf[0], i, wid)
    pathbuf[i] = 47
    i = i + 1
    k = 0
    while leaf[k] != 0:
        pathbuf[i] = leaf[k]
        i = i + 1
        k = k + 1
    pathbuf[i] = 0


def write_path(path: Ptr[char], data: Ptr[uint8]) -> int32:
    fd: int32 = sys_open_write(path)
    if fd < 0:
        return -1
    sys_write(fd, data, slen(data))
    sys_close(fd)
    return 0


def win_ctl(wid: int32, verb: Ptr[uint8]):
    winpath(wid, "ctl")
    write_path(cast[Ptr[char]](&pathbuf[0]), verb)


def newwindow() -> int32:
    if write_path(cast[Ptr[char]]("/dev/wsys/ctl"),
                  cast[Ptr[uint8]]("newwindow\n")) != 0:
        return -1
    fd: int32 = sys_open(cast[Ptr[char]]("/dev/wsys/ctl"))
    if fd < 0:
        return -1
    n: int64 = sys_read(fd, &rbuf[0], 32)
    sys_close(fd)
    if n <= 0:
        return -1
    v: int32 = 0
    i: uint64 = 0
    while i < cast[uint64](n) and rbuf[i] >= 48 and rbuf[i] <= 57:
        v = v * 10 + cast[int32](rbuf[i]) - 48
        i = i + 1
    if v < 2:
        return -1
    return v


def send_title(wid: int32, c: uint64):
    i: uint64 = 0
    p: Ptr[uint8] = "title "
    k: uint64 = 0
    while p[k] != 0:
        ctlbuf[i] = p[k]
        i = i + 1
        k = k + 1
    k = 0
    while k < conn_title_len[c] and k < TITLE_CAP:
        ctlbuf[i] = conn_title[c * TITLE_CAP + k]
        i = i + 1
        k = k + 1
    if k == 0:
        ctlbuf[i] = 87                                   # "Wayland"
        ctlbuf[i + 1] = 97
        ctlbuf[i + 2] = 121
        ctlbuf[i + 3] = 108
        ctlbuf[i + 4] = 97
        ctlbuf[i + 5] = 110
        ctlbuf[i + 6] = 100
        i = i + 7
    ctlbuf[i] = 10
    ctlbuf[i + 1] = 0
    win_ctl(wid, &ctlbuf[0])


def send_geometry(wid: int32, x: int32, y: int32, w: int32, h: int32):
    i: uint64 = 0
    p: Ptr[uint8] = "geometry "
    k: uint64 = 0
    while p[k] != 0:
        ctlbuf[i] = p[k]
        i = i + 1
        k = k + 1
    i = emit_int(&ctlbuf[0], i, x)
    ctlbuf[i] = 32
    i = emit_int(&ctlbuf[0], i + 1, y)
    ctlbuf[i] = 32
    i = emit_int(&ctlbuf[0], i + 1, w)
    ctlbuf[i] = 32
    i = emit_int(&ctlbuf[0], i + 1, h)
    ctlbuf[i] = 10
    ctlbuf[i + 1] = 0
    win_ctl(wid, &ctlbuf[0])


def win_slot(wid: int32) -> int32:
    i: uint64 = 0
    while i < n_win:
        if w_wid[i] == wid:
            return cast[int32](i)
        i = i + 1
    return -1


# Give a surface a real Hamnix window. GEOMETRY GOES FIRST because
# user/linux-wsys.c's bb_for() sizes the v2 backbuffer from the window's w/h
# the first time a blit arrives and never resizes it -- a window that is still
# its default 640x480 when the first blit lands is stuck at that stride.
# px/py < 0 means "you place it": a toplevel gets the cascade. A popup arrives
# with a position already worked out from its positioner and must land exactly
# there, undecorated -- a menu with a title bar is not a menu.
def win_open(c: uint64, surf: uint32, w: int32, h: int32,
             px: int32, py: int32, decor: int32) -> int32:
    if n_win >= MAXWIN:
        return -1
    wid: int32 = newwindow()
    if wid < 2:
        return -1
    # Cascade far enough that two windows do not sit on top of each other.
    # There is no placement policy here worth the name -- wsysd owns stacking
    # and the user can move things -- but a new toplevel landing 28 pixels
    # from the last one reads as a rendering bug rather than as two windows.
    x: int32 = px
    y: int32 = py
    if px < 0 or py < 0:
        x = 60 + cascade % 3 * 340
        y = 60 + cascade / 3 % 2 * 340
        cascade = cascade + 1
    if x + w > scr_w:
        x = scr_w - w - 20
    if y + h > scr_h:
        y = scr_h - h - 20
    if x < 0:
        x = 0
    if y < 0:
        y = 0
    send_geometry(wid, x, y, w, h)
    if decor != 0:
        win_ctl(wid, cast[Ptr[uint8]]("decorate 1\n"))
    else:
        win_ctl(wid, cast[Ptr[uint8]]("decorate 0\n"))
    # A popup is BY DEFINITION on top of the window it came from, so it goes
    # one z-band higher; a menu that opens behind its own menu bar is worse
    # than no menu at all.
    if decor != 0:
        win_ctl(wid, cast[Ptr[uint8]]("z 7\n"))
    else:
        win_ctl(wid, cast[Ptr[uint8]]("z 8\n"))
    send_title(wid, c)
    win_ctl(wid, cast[Ptr[uint8]]("version 2\n"))
    winpath(wid, "draw/ctl")
    dfd: int32 = sys_open_write(cast[Ptr[char]](&pathbuf[0]))
    if dfd < 0:
        return -1
    winpath(wid, "keys")
    kfd: int32 = sys_open(cast[Ptr[char]](&pathbuf[0]))
    winpath(wid, "pointer")
    pfd: int32 = sys_open(cast[Ptr[char]](&pathbuf[0]))
    w_wid[n_win] = wid
    w_draw[n_win] = dfd
    w_keys[n_win] = kfd
    w_ptr[n_win] = pfd
    w_conn[n_win] = cast[int32](c)
    w_surf[n_win] = cast[int32](surf)
    w_btn[n_win] = 0
    w_in[n_win] = 0
    w_focus[n_win] = 0
    w_x[n_win] = x
    w_y[n_win] = y
    n_win = n_win + 1
    puts(1, "wsyswl: surface -> wid ")
    putd(1, wid)
    puts(1, "\n")
    return wid


def win_close(wid: int32):
    s: int32 = win_slot(wid)
    if s < 0:
        return
    i: uint64 = cast[uint64](s)
    if w_draw[i] >= 0:
        sys_close(w_draw[i])
    if w_keys[i] >= 0:
        sys_close(w_keys[i])
    if w_ptr[i] >= 0:
        sys_close(w_ptr[i])
    # sys_wsys_free, not a `close <wid>` on the global ctl. Only the syscall
    # releases the window's v2 backbuffer slot, and user/linux-wsys.c has
    # THREE of them for the whole system -- a leaked slot is a window that can
    # never be drawn into again, which shows up much later as a client that
    # renders nothing for no visible reason.
    sys_wsys_free(wid)
    k: uint64 = i
    while k + 1 < n_win:
        w_wid[k] = w_wid[k + 1]
        w_draw[k] = w_draw[k + 1]
        w_keys[k] = w_keys[k + 1]
        w_ptr[k] = w_ptr[k + 1]
        w_conn[k] = w_conn[k + 1]
        w_surf[k] = w_surf[k + 1]
        w_btn[k] = w_btn[k + 1]
        w_in[k] = w_in[k + 1]
        w_focus[k] = w_focus[k + 1]
        w_x[k] = w_x[k + 1]
        w_y[k] = w_y[k + 1]
        k = k + 1
    n_win = n_win - 1


# --- shm buffer -> the window's backbuffer ----------------------------

def commit_buffer(c: uint64, surface_id: uint32, buf_id: uint32):
    if obj_get_type(c, buf_id) != T_BUFFER:
        drop(S_NOTBUF, "the attached object is not a wl_buffer this server knows")
        return
    bi: uint64 = oidx(c, buf_id)
    mi:     int32 = obj_g[bi]
    offset: int32 = obj_b[bi]
    width:  int32 = obj_c[bi]
    height: int32 = obj_d[bi]
    stride: int32 = obj_e[bi]
    if verbose != 0:
        puts(1, "wsyswl: commit_buffer map ")
        putd(1, mi)
        puts(1, " ")
        putd(1, width)
        puts(1, "x")
        putd(1, height)
        puts(1, "\n")
    if width < 1 or height < 1:
        drop(S_BADDIM, "a wl_buffer with no width or no height")
        return
    if mi < 0:
        drop(S_NOMAP, "the wl_buffer has no shm mapping (map_alloc failed earlier)")
        return
    base: uint64 = map_addr[c * MAXMAP + cast[uint64](mi)]
    maplen: uint64 = map_len[c * MAXMAP + cast[uint64](mi)]
    if base == 0:
        drop(S_NOBASE, "the shm mapping behind this wl_buffer is not mapped")
        return

    # A wl_subsurface's pixels belong to the toplevel that owns the window --
    # the child never gets a window of its own. Walk up so a client that draws
    # its content into a subsurface (Firefox does) still lands somewhere.
    eff: uint32 = surface_id
    guard: int32 = 0
    while guard < 4:
        p: int32 = surf_parent[oidx(c, eff)]
        if p == 0 or obj_get_type(c, cast[uint32](p)) != T_SURFACE:
            guard = 4
        else:
            eff = cast[uint32](p)
            guard = guard + 1
    si: uint64 = oidx(c, eff)
    wid: int32 = obj_a[si]
    if wid <= 0:
        # A popup already knows where it goes -- its xdg_surface carries the
        # screen position worked out from the positioner at get_popup time --
        # and it goes there bare. Everything else is a toplevel and cascades.
        px: int32 = -1
        py: int32 = -1
        decor: int32 = 1
        role: int32 = obj_f[si]
        if role == 0 or obj_get_type(c, cast[uint32](role)) != T_XDG_SURF:
            # A SURFACE WITHOUT A ROLE IS NOT A WINDOW. wl_pointer.set_cursor
            # takes an ordinary wl_surface and the client then attaches a
            # cursor image to it and commits, which is indistinguishable from
            # a window's first frame except for this: it never got an
            # xdg_surface. Before this check, moving the mouse over a client
            # opened a second 24x24 Hamnix window containing an arrow.
            hit(S_NOROLE)
            return
        ri: uint64 = oidx(c, cast[uint32](role))
        if obj_e[ri] == 1:
            px = obj_h[ri]
            py = obj_i[ri]
            decor = 0
        wid = win_open(c, eff, width, height, px, py, decor)
        if wid < 0:
            drop(S_NOWIN, "no wsys window could be opened for this surface")
            return
        obj_a[si] = wid
        high(S_WINHI, cast[int32](n_win))
    elif obj_c[si] != width or obj_d[si] != height:
        # A resize is a geometry announcement and nothing else: linux-wsys.c's
        # `geometry` verb calls bb_resize, which re-fits the v2 backbuffer to
        # the new size, so the stride follows the client. The window keeps the
        # corner it was placed at -- re-announcing (60,60) would walk a popup
        # away from the menu item that opened it.
        rs: int32 = win_slot(wid)
        if rs >= 0:
            send_geometry(wid, w_x[cast[uint64](rs)], w_y[cast[uint64](rs)],
                          width, height)
    obj_c[si] = width
    obj_d[si] = height

    slot: int32 = win_slot(wid)
    if slot < 0:
        drop(S_NOSLOT, "the surface's window has no wsysd slot any more")
        return
    dfd: int32 = w_draw[cast[uint64](slot)]

    rowbytes: uint64 = cast[uint64](width) * 4
    if rowbytes > 8192:
        rowbytes = 8192
    cols: int32 = cast[int32](rowbytes / 4)
    src: Ptr[uint8] = cast[Ptr[uint8]](base)
    y: int32 = 0
    while y < height:
        pos: uint64 = cast[uint64](offset) \
                    + cast[uint64](y) * cast[uint64](stride)
        if pos + rowbytes > maplen:
            # The row is off the end of what we have mapped. Everything below
            # it is too, so the rest of the window is simply not sent -- which
            # is a HALF-DRAWN window, not a missing one, and worth its own name.
            drop(S_SHORT, "a buffer row past the end of the shm mapping -- window cut short")
            y = height
        else:
            i: uint64 = 0
            while i < rowbytes:
                rowbuf[i] = src[pos + i]
                i = i + 1
            # ALPHA IS FORCED OPAQUE, for both formats. XRGB8888's fourth byte
            # is undefined and would show as transparent; ARGB8888's is real,
            # but a client's translucency is measured against ITS idea of what
            # is behind the window, and here that is the Hamnix desktop, which
            # it has never seen. An opaque window is the honest rendering of a
            # surface whose background we cannot reproduce.
            k: int32 = 0
            while k < cols:
                rowbuf[cast[uint64](k) * 4 + 3] = 255
                k = k + 1
            blithdr[0] = 66                              # 'B'
            put_le32(&blithdr[0], 1, 0)
            put_le32(&blithdr[0], 5, y)
            put_le32(&blithdr[0], 9, cols)
            put_le32(&blithdr[0], 13, y + 1)
            blithdr[17] = FMT_BGRA8888
            sys_write(dfd, &blithdr[0], 18)
            sys_write(dfd, &rowbuf[0], rowbytes)
            hit(S_ROWS)
            y = y + 1
    blithdr[0] = 68                                      # 'D', publish
    put_le32(&blithdr[0], 1, 0)
    put_le32(&blithdr[0], 5, 0)
    put_le32(&blithdr[0], 9, cols)
    put_le32(&blithdr[0], 13, height)
    sys_write(dfd, &blithdr[0], 17)
    hit(S_COMMIT)
    if verbose != 0:
        puts(1, "wsyswl: commit ")
        putd(1, width)
        puts(1, "x")
        putd(1, height)
        puts(1, " -> wid ")
        putd(1, wid)
        puts(1, "\n")


# --- request dispatch, one function per interface ---------------------

def do_display(c: uint64, opcode: uint32, args: Ptr[uint8], arglen: uint64):
    if opcode == 0:
        # sync(callback): answer immediately. Everything this server does is
        # synchronous inside one pump, so a roundtrip has nothing to wait for.
        if arglen >= 4:
            cb: uint32 = ru32(args, 0)
            new_obj(c, cb, T_CALLBACK)
            serial = serial + 1
            send_callback_done(cb, serial)
            send_delete_id(cb)
            free_obj(c, cb)
    elif opcode == 1:
        if arglen >= 4:
            reg: uint32 = ru32(args, 0)
            new_obj(c, reg, T_REGISTRY)
            conn_reg[c] = cast[int32](reg)
            send_global(reg, G_COMPOSITOR, "wl_compositor", 4)
            send_global(reg, G_SHM, "wl_shm", 1)
            send_global(reg, G_XDG, "xdg_wm_base", 1)
            send_global(reg, G_SEAT, "wl_seat", SEAT_VERSION)
            send_global(reg, G_OUTPUT, "wl_output", OUTPUT_VERSION)
            send_global(reg, G_DDM, "wl_data_device_manager", 3)
            send_global(reg, G_SUBCOMPOSITOR, "wl_subcompositor", 1)
            send_global(reg, G_VIEWPORTER, "wp_viewporter", 1)
            # v2 and not v3: at v3 the xdg_output events are latched by
            # wl_output.done rather than by zxdg_output_v1.done, and this
            # server sends the whole output description once and never
            # changes it, so the older latch is the one it can honour.
            send_global(reg, G_XDG_OUTPUT, "zxdg_output_manager_v1", 2)


def do_registry(c: uint64, args: Ptr[uint8], arglen: uint64):
    # bind(name, interface(string), version, new_id)
    if arglen < 12:
        return
    name: uint32 = ru32(args, 0)
    ilen: uint32 = ru32(args, 4)
    pad: uint64 = (cast[uint64](ilen) + 3) / 4 * 4
    after: uint64 = 8 + pad
    if arglen < after + 8:
        return
    bver:  uint32 = ru32(args, after)
    newid: uint32 = ru32(args, after + 4)
    if name == G_COMPOSITOR:
        new_obj(c, newid, T_COMPOSITOR)
    elif name == G_SHM:
        new_obj(c, newid, T_SHM)
        send_shm_format(newid, SHM_ARGB8888)
        send_shm_format(newid, SHM_XRGB8888)
    elif name == G_XDG:
        new_obj(c, newid, T_XDG_WM)
        conn_xdg_wm[c] = cast[int32](newid)
    elif name == G_SEAT:
        new_obj(c, newid, T_SEAT)
        conn_seat[c] = cast[int32](newid)
        obj_a[oidx(c, newid)] = cast[int32](bver)
        send_seat_caps(newid, SEAT_CAP_POINTER | SEAT_CAP_KEYBOARD)
        # wl_seat.name is a v2 event. A client that bound at v1 has no
        # listener slot for it and libwayland ABORTS on an event it cannot
        # dispatch, so this is gated rather than always sent.
        if bver >= 2:
            send_seat_name(newid, "seat0")
    elif name == G_OUTPUT:
        new_obj(c, newid, T_OUTPUT)
        conn_output[c] = cast[int32](newid)
        send_output_all(newid)
    elif name == G_DDM:
        new_obj(c, newid, T_DDM)
    elif name == G_SUBCOMPOSITOR:
        new_obj(c, newid, T_SUBCOMPOSITOR)
    elif name == G_VIEWPORTER:
        new_obj(c, newid, T_VIEWPORTER)
    elif name == G_XDG_OUTPUT:
        new_obj(c, newid, T_XDG_OUT_MGR)


def do_seat(c: uint64, obj: uint32, opcode: uint32, args: Ptr[uint8],
            arglen: uint64):
    if opcode == 0:
        if arglen >= 4:
            p: uint32 = ru32(args, 0)
            new_obj(c, p, T_POINTER)
            # THE FIRST ONE WINS. A client may bind wl_seat more than once --
            # Firefox binds it through two registries -- and only put a
            # listener on the first. libwayland silently DISCARDS an event
            # addressed to a proxy with no listener, so aiming at the newest
            # wl_pointer produced a browser that rendered perfectly and could
            # not be clicked, with nothing logged anywhere. The first seat a
            # toolkit binds is the one it is actually using.
            if conn_pointer[c] == 0:
                conn_pointer[c] = cast[int32](p)
    elif opcode == 1:
        if arglen >= 4:
            k: uint32 = ru32(args, 0)
            new_obj(c, k, T_KEYBOARD)
            if conn_keyboard[c] == 0:            # the first one wins; see above
                conn_keyboard[c] = cast[int32](k)
            build_keymap()
            if keymap_fd >= 0:
                # The fd rides out with the keymap event itself: the client's
                # recvmsg has to see them together or it has a size and no
                # descriptor. out_fd is consumed by the next flush.
                out_fd = keymap_fd
                keymap_fd = -1
                send_kbd_keymap(k, cast[uint32](keymap_len))
                build_keymap()                  # rebuild for the next client
            sver: int32 = obj_a[oidx(c, obj)]
            if sver >= 4:
                send_kbd_repeat_info(k, 25, 600)


# After a wl_pointer or wl_keyboard goes away, find another one on the same
# connection, or give up.
#
# THIS IS NOT HOUSEKEEPING. A wl_pointer.release frees the object id, and the
# client immediately RE-USES that number for the next thing it makes -- in
# Firefox's case a wl_region. Keeping the released id in conn_pointer meant
# every subsequent motion and click was addressed to a region, which libwayland
# silently drops: the browser rendered perfectly and could not be clicked, with
# no error anywhere. Firefox releases a pointer because it binds wl_seat twice,
# through two registries, and tidies the second one up.
def rebind_input(c: uint64, want: int32) -> int32:
    i: uint64 = MAXOBJ
    while i > 0:
        i = i - 1
        if obj_type[c * MAXOBJ + i] == want:
            return cast[int32](i)
    return 0


def do_shm(c: uint64, opcode: uint32, args: Ptr[uint8], arglen: uint64):
    if opcode != 0:
        return
    # create_pool(id, fd, size). The fd argument is not in `args` -- it came
    # over the socket as ancillary data and is waiting at the head of this
    # connection's descriptor queue.
    if arglen < 8:
        return
    nfd: int32 = fdq_pop(c)
    poolid: uint32 = ru32(args, 0)
    size:   uint32 = ru32(args, 4)
    new_obj(c, poolid, T_SHM_POOL)
    if valid_id(poolid) == 0:
        return
    pi: uint64 = oidx(c, poolid)
    obj_a[pi] = nfd
    if verbose != 0:
        puts(1, "wsyswl: create_pool fd ")
        putd(1, nfd)
        puts(1, "\n")
    if nfd < 0:
        return
    mi: int32 = map_alloc(c)
    if mi < 0:
        sys_close(nfd)
        obj_a[pi] = -1
        return
    # The DESCRIPTOR is the authority on how much is safe to map, not the size
    # in the request: a later wl_shm_pool.resize grows the pool without
    # re-sending the fd, and mapping past the end of the memfd is a SIGBUS on
    # the first touched page.
    fsz: int64 = sys_fd_size(nfd)
    want: uint64 = cast[uint64](size)
    if fsz > 0 and cast[uint64](fsz) > want:
        want = cast[uint64](fsz)
    m: uint64 = c * MAXMAP + cast[uint64](mi)
    map_addr[m] = sys_mmap_shared(nfd, want)
    map_len[m] = want
    obj_g[pi] = mi
    obj_b[pi] = cast[int32](size)


def do_shm_pool(c: uint64, obj: uint32, opcode: uint32, args: Ptr[uint8],
                arglen: uint64):
    pi: uint64 = oidx(c, obj)
    if opcode == 0:
        # create_buffer(id, offset, width, height, stride, format)
        if arglen >= 24:
            bufid: uint32 = ru32(args, 0)
            new_obj(c, bufid, T_BUFFER)
            if valid_id(bufid) != 0:
                bi: uint64 = oidx(c, bufid)
                obj_a[bi] = cast[int32](obj)             # the pool it came from
                obj_b[bi] = cast[int32](ru32(args, 4))
                obj_c[bi] = cast[int32](ru32(args, 8))
                obj_d[bi] = cast[int32](ru32(args, 12))
                obj_e[bi] = cast[int32](ru32(args, 16))
                obj_f[bi] = cast[int32](ru32(args, 20))
                obj_g[bi] = obj_g[pi]                    # share the mapping
                map_ref_up(c, obj_g[pi])                 # and hold it open
    elif opcode == 1:
        # destroy: the pool object goes, the MAPPING stays -- the buffers cut
        # from it are still live and are the whole reason it was created.
        if obj_a[pi] >= 0:
            sys_close(obj_a[pi])
            obj_a[pi] = -1
        map_unref(c, obj_g[pi])                  # drop the pool's reference
        obj_g[pi] = -1
        free_obj(c, obj)
    elif opcode == 2:
        # resize(size): remap, because the pages past the old end are not in
        # our mapping at all. Buffers carved before the resize keep working:
        # they hold the same map index, which now points at the new address.
        if arglen >= 4:
            mi: int32 = obj_g[pi]
            ns: uint64 = cast[uint64](ru32(args, 0))
            if mi >= 0 and obj_a[pi] >= 0:
                m: uint64 = c * MAXMAP + cast[uint64](mi)
                if ns > map_len[m]:
                    if map_addr[m] != 0:
                        sys_munmap_at(map_addr[m], map_len[m])
                    map_addr[m] = sys_mmap_shared(obj_a[pi], ns)
                    map_len[m] = ns
            obj_b[pi] = cast[int32](ns)


def do_surface(c: uint64, obj: uint32, opcode: uint32, args: Ptr[uint8],
               arglen: uint64):
    si: uint64 = oidx(c, obj)
    if opcode == 1:
        # attach(buffer, x, y)
        if arglen >= 4:
            obj_b[si] = cast[int32](ru32(args, 0))
    elif opcode == 3:
        # frame(callback): queue it against this surface; it is armed by the
        # next commit of this surface and answered then, together with every
        # other callback queued on it.
        if arglen >= 4:
            fcb: uint32 = ru32(args, 0)
            new_obj(c, fcb, T_CALLBACK)
            if fc_add(c, obj, fcb) == 0:
                # The table is full, which means a client is asking for frames
                # faster than it commits. Answering now is wrong by a frame and
                # right by everything else -- a dropped callback stalls a
                # client permanently.
                hit(S_FCFULL)
                evt_time = evt_time + 16
                send_callback_done(fcb, evt_time)
                send_delete_id(fcb)
                free_obj(c, fcb)
    elif opcode == 6:
        # commit. An xdg_surface's buffer only maps AFTER the client has acked
        # a configure, and the spec is strict about when that configure may be
        # sent: only IN RESPONSE TO the client's first, bufferless commit. GTK
        # (so Firefox, so XWayland) ignores a configure that arrives before it
        # and then waits forever for the one that never comes -- which looks
        # exactly like a client that failed to start.
        if obj_g[si] < 0:
            # First commit on this surface: it is now on the one output there
            # is. obj_g doubles as the "already told" flag -- enter must not
            # repeat, a client keeps a list and would end up on the same
            # monitor twice.
            outp: int32 = conn_output[c]
            if outp != 0 and obj_get_type(c, cast[uint32](outp)) == T_OUTPUT:
                send_surface_enter(obj, cast[uint32](outp))
            obj_g[si] = 1
        xrole: int32 = obj_f[si]
        configured: int32 = 1
        if xrole != 0 and obj_get_type(c, cast[uint32](xrole)) == T_XDG_SURF:
            xi: uint64 = oidx(c, cast[uint32](xrole))
            configured = obj_c[xi]
            if configured == 0 and obj_b[xi] == 0:
                popid: int32 = obj_g[xi]
                if obj_e[xi] == 1 and popid > 0 \
                        and obj_get_type(c, cast[uint32](popid)) == T_XDG_POPUP:
                    pp: uint64 = oidx(c, cast[uint32](popid))
                    send_popup_configure(cast[uint32](popid), obj_d[pp],
                                         obj_e[pp], obj_b[pp], obj_c[pp])
                tid: int32 = obj_d[xi]
                if tid != 0:
                    cw: int32 = 0
                    ch: int32 = 0
                    maxed: int32 = obj_b[oidx(c, cast[uint32](tid))]
                    if maxed != 0:
                        cw = MAX_W
                        ch = MAX_H
                    send_toplevel_configure(cast[uint32](tid),
                                            cast[uint32](cw), cast[uint32](ch),
                                            maxed)
                serial = serial + 1
                send_xdg_configure(cast[uint32](xrole), serial)
                obj_b[xi] = cast[int32](serial)
        pend: int32 = obj_b[si]
        if pend != 0:
            if configured != 0:
                commit_buffer(c, obj, cast[uint32](pend))
            else:
                drop(S_UNCONF,
                     "a buffer committed on an xdg_surface that never acked a configure")
            send_buffer_release(cast[uint32](pend))
            obj_b[si] = 0
        # Every frame callback queued on this surface is answered at once
        # rather than at a vblank. There is no vblank to wait for here, and a
        # client that throttles on it would otherwise never draw a second
        # frame. They all get the SAME timestamp, because they are all the
        # same frame -- a client that sees two times for one frame can compute
        # a nonsense refresh rate from the difference.
        evt_time = evt_time + 16
        fi: uint64 = 0
        while fi < FCMAX:
            if fc_conn[fi] == cast[int32](c) and fc_surf[fi] == cast[int32](obj):
                fcb2: uint32 = cast[uint32](fc_cb[fi])
                fc_conn[fi] = -1
                send_callback_done(fcb2, evt_time)
                send_delete_id(fcb2)
                free_obj(c, fcb2)
            fi = fi + 1
    elif opcode == 0:
        if obj_a[si] > 0:
            win_close(obj_a[si])
            obj_a[si] = 0
        fc_drop_surf(c, obj)
        free_obj(c, obj)


def do_xdg_wm(c: uint64, opcode: uint32, args: Ptr[uint8], arglen: uint64):
    if opcode == 2:
        # get_xdg_surface(id, surface): link both ways so commit can find the
        # role and the role can find the window.
        if arglen >= 8:
            xid: uint32 = ru32(args, 0)
            surf: uint32 = ru32(args, 4)
            new_obj(c, xid, T_XDG_SURF)
            if valid_id(xid) != 0:
                obj_a[oidx(c, xid)] = cast[int32](surf)
            if obj_get_type(c, surf) == T_SURFACE:
                obj_f[oidx(c, surf)] = cast[int32](xid)
    elif opcode == 1:
        # create_positioner(id)
        if arglen >= 4:
            pid: uint32 = ru32(args, 0)
            new_obj(c, pid, T_POSITIONER)
            if valid_id(pid) != 0:
                obj_g[oidx(c, pid)] = 0     # anchor and gravity, both "none"


def do_positioner(c: uint64, obj: uint32, opcode: uint32, args: Ptr[uint8],
                  arglen: uint64):
    pi: uint64 = oidx(c, obj)
    if opcode == 1 and arglen >= 8:              # set_size(w, h)
        obj_a[pi] = cast[int32](ru32(args, 0))
        obj_b[pi] = cast[int32](ru32(args, 4))
    elif opcode == 2 and arglen >= 16:           # set_anchor_rect(x, y, w, h)
        obj_c[pi] = cast[int32](ru32(args, 0))
        obj_d[pi] = cast[int32](ru32(args, 4))
        obj_e[pi] = cast[int32](ru32(args, 8))
        obj_f[pi] = cast[int32](ru32(args, 12))
    elif opcode == 3 and arglen >= 4:            # set_anchor(anchor)
        obj_g[pi] = (obj_g[pi] & 0xff00) | (cast[int32](ru32(args, 0)) & 255)
    elif opcode == 4 and arglen >= 4:            # set_gravity(gravity)
        obj_g[pi] = (obj_g[pi] & 255) \
                  | ((cast[int32](ru32(args, 0)) & 255) << 8)
    elif opcode == 6 and arglen >= 8:            # set_offset(x, y)
        obj_h[pi] = cast[int32](ru32(args, 0))
        obj_i[pi] = cast[int32](ru32(args, 4))


# Where a popup goes, in the coordinates of its parent xdg_surface's window
# geometry. This is the whole of xdg_positioner that a menu actually uses:
# take a point on the anchor rectangle chosen by ANCHOR, then hang the popup
# off that point in the direction given by GRAVITY, then add the offset.
#
# The constraint-adjustment rules (flip, slide, resize when the popup would
# leave the screen) are NOT implemented -- win_open clamps the popup onto the
# screen instead, which slides it without telling the client. A menu near the
# right edge therefore overlaps its parent item rather than flipping to its
# left.
#
# The anchor/gravity enumerations are the same eight values in the same order:
# 1 top, 2 bottom, 3 left, 4 right, 5 top_left, 6 bottom_left, 7 top_right,
# 8 bottom_right; 0 is "centre", and so is anything unrecognised.
def anchor_is_left(a: int32) -> int32:
    if a == 3 or a == 5 or a == 6:
        return 1
    return 0


def anchor_is_right(a: int32) -> int32:
    if a == 4 or a == 7 or a == 8:
        return 1
    return 0


def anchor_is_top(a: int32) -> int32:
    if a == 1 or a == 5 or a == 7:
        return 1
    return 0


def anchor_is_bottom(a: int32) -> int32:
    if a == 2 or a == 6 or a == 8:
        return 1
    return 0


def popup_x(c: uint64, pos: uint32) -> int32:
    pi: uint64 = oidx(c, pos)
    a: int32 = obj_g[pi] & 255
    g: int32 = (obj_g[pi] >> 8) & 255
    x: int32 = obj_c[pi] + obj_e[pi] / 2
    if anchor_is_left(a) != 0:
        x = obj_c[pi]
    elif anchor_is_right(a) != 0:
        x = obj_c[pi] + obj_e[pi]
    if anchor_is_left(g) != 0:
        x = x - obj_a[pi]
    elif anchor_is_right(g) == 0:
        x = x - obj_a[pi] / 2
    return x + obj_h[pi]


def popup_y(c: uint64, pos: uint32) -> int32:
    pi: uint64 = oidx(c, pos)
    a: int32 = obj_g[pi] & 255
    g: int32 = (obj_g[pi] >> 8) & 255
    y: int32 = obj_d[pi] + obj_f[pi] / 2
    if anchor_is_top(a) != 0:
        y = obj_d[pi]
    elif anchor_is_bottom(a) != 0:
        y = obj_d[pi] + obj_f[pi]
    if anchor_is_top(g) != 0:
        y = y - obj_b[pi]
    elif anchor_is_bottom(g) == 0:
        y = y - obj_b[pi] / 2
    return y + obj_i[pi]


def send_popup_configure(pop: uint32, x: int32, y: int32, w: int32, h: int32):
    s: uint64 = emit_begin(pop)
    o_u32(cast[uint32](x))
    o_u32(cast[uint32](y))
    o_u32(cast[uint32](w))
    o_u32(cast[uint32](h))
    emit_end(s, 0)


def do_xdg_surf(c: uint64, obj: uint32, opcode: uint32, args: Ptr[uint8],
                arglen: uint64):
    xi: uint64 = oidx(c, obj)
    if opcode == 1:
        # get_toplevel(id). No configure here -- see the commit handler.
        if arglen >= 4:
            tid: uint32 = ru32(args, 0)
            new_obj(c, tid, T_XDG_TOP)
            if valid_id(tid) != 0:
                obj_a[oidx(c, tid)] = cast[int32](obj)
            obj_d[xi] = cast[int32](tid)
    elif opcode == 2:
        # get_popup(id, parent, positioner). THIS xdg_surface takes the popup
        # role; `id` is the xdg_popup handle for it. The position is worked out
        # once, here, and stashed on the xdg_surface, because by the time a
        # buffer arrives the positioner may already have been destroyed -- a
        # client is free to destroy it as soon as the popup exists.
        if arglen >= 12:
            popid: uint32 = ru32(args, 0)
            parent: uint32 = ru32(args, 4)
            pos:    uint32 = ru32(args, 8)
            new_obj(c, popid, T_XDG_POPUP)
            if valid_id(popid) != 0:
                obj_a[oidx(c, popid)] = cast[int32](obj)
            obj_e[xi] = 1                            # this surface is a popup
            obj_f[xi] = cast[int32](parent)
            obj_g[xi] = cast[int32](popid)
            lx: int32 = 0
            ly: int32 = 0
            pw: int32 = 0
            ph: int32 = 0
            if obj_get_type(c, pos) == T_POSITIONER:
                lx = popup_x(c, pos)
                ly = popup_y(c, pos)
                pw = obj_a[oidx(c, pos)]
                ph = obj_b[oidx(c, pos)]
            # The client is told the position in ITS parent's coordinates; the
            # window is placed in screen coordinates. They differ by wherever
            # wsysd put the parent, which is why win_open records that.
            obj_h[xi] = lx
            obj_i[xi] = ly
            if obj_get_type(c, parent) == T_XDG_SURF:
                psurf: int32 = obj_a[oidx(c, parent)]
                if psurf != 0 and obj_get_type(c, cast[uint32](psurf)) == T_SURFACE:
                    pwid: int32 = obj_a[oidx(c, cast[uint32](psurf))]
                    ps: int32 = win_slot(pwid)
                    if ps >= 0:
                        obj_h[xi] = w_x[cast[uint64](ps)] + lx
                        obj_i[xi] = w_y[cast[uint64](ps)] + ly
            # No configure here, for the same reason a toplevel gets none
            # here: xdg-shell says the compositor answers the client's first,
            # bufferless commit, and a toolkit that is handed a configure
            # before it has asked drops it and waits for one that never comes.
            if valid_id(popid) != 0:
                popi: uint64 = oidx(c, popid)
                obj_b[popi] = pw
                obj_c[popi] = ph
                obj_d[popi] = lx
                obj_e[popi] = ly
    elif opcode == 4:
        # ack_configure(serial)
        obj_c[xi] = 1
        obj_b[xi] = 0


# Re-answer a toplevel whose state changed after it was already mapped.
#
# Deliberately silent before the first ack: the initial configure may only be
# sent in response to the client's first bufferless commit (see do_surface), so
# a set_maximized that arrives during window construction -- which is when
# Firefox sends it -- must be left for that path to pick up. Sending one here
# too would give the client two configures with two serials and it would ack
# the wrong one.
def reconfigure(c: uint64, top: uint32, xsid: uint32):
    if obj_get_type(c, xsid) != T_XDG_SURF:
        return
    xi: uint64 = oidx(c, xsid)
    if obj_c[xi] == 0:
        return
    maxed: int32 = obj_b[oidx(c, top)]
    cw: int32 = 0
    ch: int32 = 0
    if maxed != 0:
        cw = MAX_W
        ch = MAX_H
    send_toplevel_configure(top, cast[uint32](cw), cast[uint32](ch), maxed)
    serial = serial + 1
    send_xdg_configure(xsid, serial)


def do_xdg_top(c: uint64, obj: uint32, opcode: uint32, args: Ptr[uint8],
               arglen: uint64):
    ti: uint64 = oidx(c, obj)
    xsid: int32 = obj_a[ti]
    if opcode == 2:
        # set_title(title). It usually arrives before the window exists, so it
        # is stashed on the connection and applied at window-open time; if the
        # window is already up, push it now.
        if arglen >= 4:
            tlen: uint32 = ru32(args, 0)
            n: uint64 = 0
            while n + 1 < TITLE_CAP and n + 4 < arglen \
                  and cast[uint32](n) < tlen and args[4 + n] != 0:
                conn_title[c * TITLE_CAP + n] = args[4 + n]
                n = n + 1
            conn_title_len[c] = n
            if obj_get_type(c, cast[uint32](xsid)) == T_XDG_SURF:
                surf: int32 = obj_a[oidx(c, cast[uint32](xsid))]
                if surf != 0 and obj_get_type(c, cast[uint32](surf)) == T_SURFACE:
                    wid: int32 = obj_a[oidx(c, cast[uint32](surf))]
                    if wid > 0:
                        send_title(wid, c)
    elif opcode == 9 or opcode == 11:
        # set_maximized / set_fullscreen. Both are answered the same way: this
        # desktop has one size for a big window and no fullscreen mode of its
        # own. What matters is that the answer ARRIVES -- see
        # send_toplevel_configure.
        obj_b[ti] = 1
        reconfigure(c, obj, cast[uint32](xsid))
    elif opcode == 10 or opcode == 12:
        # unset_maximized / unset_fullscreen: give the size back to the client.
        obj_b[ti] = 0
        reconfigure(c, obj, cast[uint32](xsid))
    elif opcode == 0:
        # destroy: the window goes with the role.
        if obj_get_type(c, cast[uint32](xsid)) == T_XDG_SURF:
            surf2: int32 = obj_a[oidx(c, cast[uint32](xsid))]
            if surf2 != 0 and obj_get_type(c, cast[uint32](surf2)) == T_SURFACE:
                wid2: int32 = obj_a[oidx(c, cast[uint32](surf2))]
                if wid2 > 0:
                    win_close(wid2)
                    obj_a[oidx(c, cast[uint32](surf2))] = 0
        free_obj(c, obj)


def do_subcompositor(c: uint64, opcode: uint32, args: Ptr[uint8],
                     arglen: uint64):
    if opcode == 1:
        # get_subsurface(id, surface, parent)
        if arglen >= 12:
            subid: uint32 = ru32(args, 0)
            ch: uint32 = ru32(args, 4)
            pa: uint32 = ru32(args, 8)
            new_obj(c, subid, T_SUBSURFACE)
            if valid_id(subid) != 0:
                si: uint64 = oidx(c, subid)
                obj_a[si] = cast[int32](ch)
                obj_b[si] = cast[int32](pa)
            if obj_get_type(c, ch) == T_SURFACE \
                    and obj_get_type(c, pa) == T_SURFACE:
                surf_parent[oidx(c, ch)] = cast[int32](pa)


# wp_viewporter: the object graph, and nothing behind it.
#
# A viewport crops (set_source) and scales (set_destination) a surface's buffer
# on its way to the screen. Neither is needed here -- everything runs at scale
# 1 and a client's destination is its buffer size -- but the GLOBAL has to
# EXIST, because a toolkit that means to scale through a viewport and cannot
# find one does not fall back, it stops. Firefox creates a viewport for its
# content subsurface as part of bringing the window up.
#
# If a client ever asks for a destination that is not its buffer size, this
# silently draws the buffer instead. Scaling in the blit path is the fix, and
# is a real piece of work, not a line.
def do_viewporter(c: uint64, opcode: uint32, args: Ptr[uint8], arglen: uint64):
    if opcode == 1:
        # get_viewport(id, surface)
        if arglen >= 8:
            vid: uint32 = ru32(args, 0)
            new_obj(c, vid, T_VIEWPORT)
            if valid_id(vid) != 0:
                obj_a[oidx(c, vid)] = cast[int32](ru32(args, 4))


def dispatch(c: uint64, obj: uint32, opcode: uint32, args: Ptr[uint8],
             arglen: uint64):
    otype: int32 = obj_get_type(c, obj)
    fd: int32 = conn_fd[c]
    if obj == DISPLAY_ID:
        do_display(c, opcode, args, arglen)
    elif otype == T_REGISTRY:
        if opcode == 0:
            do_registry(c, args, arglen)
    elif otype == T_COMPOSITOR:
        if opcode == 0:
            if arglen >= 4:
                new_obj(c, ru32(args, 0), T_SURFACE)
        elif opcode == 1:
            if arglen >= 4:
                new_obj(c, ru32(args, 0), T_REGION)
    elif otype == T_SHM:
        do_shm(c, opcode, args, arglen)
    elif otype == T_SHM_POOL:
        do_shm_pool(c, obj, opcode, args, arglen)
    elif otype == T_SURFACE:
        do_surface(c, obj, opcode, args, arglen)
    elif otype == T_XDG_WM:
        if opcode == 2 or opcode == 1:
            do_xdg_wm(c, opcode, args, arglen)
    elif otype == T_XDG_SURF:
        do_xdg_surf(c, obj, opcode, args, arglen)
    elif otype == T_XDG_TOP:
        do_xdg_top(c, obj, opcode, args, arglen)
    elif otype == T_POSITIONER:
        do_positioner(c, obj, opcode, args, arglen)
    elif otype == T_XDG_POPUP:
        if opcode == 0:
            # destroy: take the menu off the screen with it. Nothing else
            # closes an undecorated window -- there is no frame to click.
            xs: int32 = obj_a[oidx(c, obj)]
            if obj_get_type(c, cast[uint32](xs)) == T_XDG_SURF:
                ps2: int32 = obj_a[oidx(c, cast[uint32](xs))]
                if ps2 != 0 and obj_get_type(c, cast[uint32](ps2)) == T_SURFACE:
                    pw2: int32 = obj_a[oidx(c, cast[uint32](ps2))]
                    if pw2 > 0:
                        win_close(pw2)
                        obj_a[oidx(c, cast[uint32](ps2))] = 0
            free_obj(c, obj)
    elif otype == T_SEAT:
        do_seat(c, obj, opcode, args, arglen)
    elif otype == T_POINTER:
        if opcode == 1:                              # release
            free_obj(c, obj)
            if conn_pointer[c] == cast[int32](obj):
                conn_pointer[c] = rebind_input(c, T_POINTER)
    elif otype == T_KEYBOARD:
        if opcode == 0:                              # release
            free_obj(c, obj)
            if conn_keyboard[c] == cast[int32](obj):
                conn_keyboard[c] = rebind_input(c, T_KEYBOARD)
    elif otype == T_SUBCOMPOSITOR:
        do_subcompositor(c, opcode, args, arglen)
    elif otype == T_VIEWPORTER:
        do_viewporter(c, opcode, args, arglen)
    elif otype == T_VIEWPORT:
        if opcode == 0:
            free_obj(c, obj)
    elif otype == T_XDG_OUT_MGR:
        if opcode == 1:
            # get_xdg_output(id, output)
            if arglen >= 8:
                xo: uint32 = ru32(args, 0)
                new_obj(c, xo, T_XDG_OUT)
                send_xdg_output_all(xo)
    elif otype == T_XDG_OUT:
        if opcode == 0:
            free_obj(c, obj)
    elif otype == T_DDM:
        if opcode == 0:
            if arglen >= 4:
                new_obj(c, ru32(args, 0), T_DATA_SOURCE)
        elif opcode == 1:
            if arglen >= 4:
                new_obj(c, ru32(args, 0), T_DATA_DEVICE)
    elif otype == T_BUFFER:
        if opcode == 0:
            map_unref(c, obj_g[oidx(c, obj)])
            obj_g[oidx(c, obj)] = -1
            free_obj(c, obj)
            send_delete_id(obj)
    elif otype == T_OUTPUT:
        if opcode == 0:
            free_obj(c, obj)
    # Anything else -- region, callback, data device, an opcode from a newer
    # protocol version -- is consumed silently. A server that killed the
    # connection over an unknown request would break on every toolkit update.
    flush(fd)


# --- connections ------------------------------------------------------

def conn_reset(c: uint64):
    conn_acclen[c] = 0
    conn_reg[c] = 0
    conn_seat[c] = 0
    conn_pointer[c] = 0
    conn_keyboard[c] = 0
    conn_xdg_wm[c] = 0
    conn_output[c] = 0
    conn_title_len[c] = 0
    fc_drop_conn(c)
    fdq_head[c] = 0
    fdq_tail[c] = 0
    i: uint64 = 0
    while i < MAXOBJ:
        obj_type[c * MAXOBJ + i] = T_NONE
        surf_parent[c * MAXOBJ + i] = 0
        obj_g[c * MAXOBJ + i] = -1
        i = i + 1
    i = 0
    while i < MAXMAP:
        map_used[c * MAXMAP + i] = 0
        map_addr[c * MAXMAP + i] = 0
        map_len[c * MAXMAP + i] = 0
        map_ref[c * MAXMAP + i] = 0
        i = i + 1
    obj_type[c * MAXOBJ + 1] = T_DISPLAY        # wl_display is always id 1


def conn_free(c: uint64):
    if conn_inuse[c] == 0:
        return
    i: uint64 = 0
    while i < MAXOBJ:
        oi: uint64 = c * MAXOBJ + i
        if obj_type[oi] == T_SHM_POOL:
            if obj_a[oi] >= 0:
                sys_close(obj_a[oi])
                obj_a[oi] = -1
        elif obj_type[oi] == T_SURFACE:
            if obj_a[oi] > 0:
                win_close(obj_a[oi])
        obj_type[oi] = T_NONE
        i = i + 1
    fc_drop_conn(c)
    while fdq_head[c] != fdq_tail[c]:
        sys_close(fdq_pop(c))
    i = 0
    while i < MAXMAP:
        m: uint64 = c * MAXMAP + i
        if map_addr[m] != 0:
            sys_munmap_at(map_addr[m], map_len[m])
            map_addr[m] = 0
        map_used[m] = 0
        map_ref[m] = 0
        i = i + 1
    sys_close(conn_fd[c])
    conn_inuse[c] = 0
    conn_fd[c] = -1
    puts(1, "wsyswl: client gone\n")


def conn_accept():
    while 1 == 1:
        fd: int32 = sys_unix_accept(listen_fd)
        if fd < 0:
            return
        c: uint64 = 0
        placed: int32 = 0
        while c < MAXCONN and placed == 0:
            if conn_inuse[c] == 0:
                conn_inuse[c] = 1
                conn_fd[c] = fd
                conn_reset(c)
                placed = 1
                puts(1, "wsyswl: client connected\n")
            c = c + 1
        if placed == 0:
            sys_close(fd)
            puts(2, "wsyswl: too many clients\n")


# Drain one client's socket and run every complete message in it.
#
# THE FD AND THE BYTES ARRIVE TOGETHER and must be kept together: the kernel
# attaches a client's shm memfd to the first byte of the sendmsg that carried
# wl_shm.create_pool, so a reader that took bytes with read(2) and went looking
# for the descriptor afterwards would find it gone. Every read here is a
# recvmsg, whether or not a descriptor is expected, and the descriptor is held
# until the create_pool in the same buffer consumes it.
def conn_pump(c: uint64) -> int32:
    fd: int32 = conn_fd[c]
    base: uint64 = c * ACC
    acclen: uint64 = conn_acclen[c]
    if acclen < ACC:
        recvn[0] = 0
        got: int64 = sys_scm_recv(fd, &conn_acc[base + acclen], ACC - acclen,
                                  &recvfds[0], 4, &recvn[0])
        if got < 0:
            return -1
        k: int32 = 0
        while k < recvn[0]:
            fdq_push(c, recvfds[k])
            k = k + 1
        acclen = acclen + cast[uint64](got)
    pos: uint64 = 0
    while acclen - pos >= 8:
        obj: uint32 = ru32(&conn_acc[base], pos)
        w2:  uint32 = ru32(&conn_acc[base], pos + 4)
        size: uint64 = cast[uint64](w2 >> 16)
        opcode: uint32 = w2 & 0xffff
        if size < 8:
            # A corrupt header cannot be resynchronised by scanning -- any
            # four bytes look like an object id. Drop the connection instead
            # of inventing a message boundary.
            conn_acclen[c] = 0
            return -1
        if acclen - pos < size:
            acclen = acclen                              # incomplete; wait
            pos = pos
            break
        dispatch(c, obj, opcode, &conn_acc[base + pos + 8], size - 8)
        pos = pos + size
    if pos > 0:
        rem: uint64 = acclen - pos
        k2: uint64 = 0
        while k2 < rem:
            conn_acc[base + k2] = conn_acc[base + pos + k2]
            k2 = k2 + 1
        acclen = rem
    conn_acclen[c] = acclen
    return 0


# --- input: the window's rings -> wl_pointer / wl_keyboard ------------
#
# wsysd routes input to a window by WRITING its /keys and /pointer rings --
# "d <code>\n" and "<kind> <x> <y> <buttons> <dz>\n" -- exactly as it does for
# a scene app. Nothing about Wayland reaches wsysd; this end reads the same two
# files any hamui client reads and turns the lines into protocol events. That
# is the whole reason a Wayland client can share a desktop with hamterm.

def parse_int_at(buf: Ptr[uint8], p: uint64, end: uint64) -> int32:
    i: uint64 = p
    neg: int32 = 0
    if i < end and buf[i] == 45:
        neg = 1
        i = i + 1
    v: int32 = 0
    while i < end and buf[i] >= 48 and buf[i] <= 57:
        v = v * 10 + cast[int32](buf[i]) - 48
        i = i + 1
    if neg != 0:
        return 0 - v
    return v


def skip_field(buf: Ptr[uint8], p: uint64, end: uint64) -> uint64:
    i: uint64 = p
    while i < end and buf[i] != 32:
        i = i + 1
    while i < end and buf[i] == 32:
        i = i + 1
    return i


# An ASCII byte from the cooked keyboard, as an evdev keycode plus whether it
# needs Shift. Returns code, or 0 for a byte with no key on a us layout.
def ascii_evdev(a: int32) -> int32:
    if a >= 97 and a <= 122:
        a = a - 32                                       # fold to uppercase
    if a >= 65 and a <= 90:
        # QWERTYUIOP ASDFGHJKL ZXCVBNM in evdev order.
        row: Ptr[uint8] = "QWERTYUIOP"
        i: int32 = 0
        while i < 10:
            if cast[int32](row[i]) == a:
                return 16 + i
            i = i + 1
        row = "ASDFGHJKL"
        i = 0
        while i < 9:
            if cast[int32](row[i]) == a:
                return 30 + i
            i = i + 1
        row = "ZXCVBNM"
        i = 0
        while i < 7:
            if cast[int32](row[i]) == a:
                return 44 + i
            i = i + 1
        return 0
    if a >= 49 and a <= 57:
        return 2 + (a - 49)                              # 1..9
    if a == 48:
        return 11                                        # 0
    if a == 32:
        return 57
    if a == 10 or a == 13:
        return 28                                        # Return
    if a == 8 or a == 127:
        return 14                                        # BackSpace
    if a == 9:
        return 15                                        # Tab
    if a == 45:
        return 12
    if a == 61:
        return 13
    if a == 59:
        return 39
    if a == 39:
        return 40
    if a == 44:
        return 51
    if a == 46:
        return 52
    if a == 47:
        return 53
    return 0


def needs_shift(a: int32) -> int32:
    if a >= 65 and a <= 90:
        return 1
    return 0


def key_tap(c: uint64, code: int32, shift: int32):
    kbd: int32 = conn_keyboard[c]
    if kbd == 0 or code == 0:
        return
    evt_time = evt_time + 8
    if shift != 0:
        serial = serial + 1
        send_kbd_modifiers(cast[uint32](kbd), serial, MOD_SHIFT)
    serial = serial + 1
    send_kbd_key(cast[uint32](kbd), serial, evt_time, cast[uint32](code),
                 STATE_PRESSED)
    evt_time = evt_time + 8
    serial = serial + 1
    send_kbd_key(cast[uint32](kbd), serial, evt_time, cast[uint32](code),
                 STATE_RELEASED)
    if shift != 0:
        serial = serial + 1
        send_kbd_modifiers(cast[uint32](kbd), serial, 0)


def btn_edges(c: uint64, i: uint64, surf: uint32, mask: int32):
    ptr: int32 = conn_pointer[c]
    if ptr == 0:
        return
    old: int32 = w_btn[i]
    b: int32 = 0
    while b < 3:
        bit: int32 = 1
        if b == 1:
            bit = 2
        elif b == 2:
            bit = 4
        code: uint32 = BTN_LEFT
        if b == 1:
            code = BTN_MIDDLE
        elif b == 2:
            code = BTN_RIGHT
        was: int32 = old & bit
        now: int32 = mask & bit
        if was != now:
            st: uint32 = STATE_RELEASED
            if now != 0:
                st = STATE_PRESSED
            serial = serial + 1
            evt_time = evt_time + 4
            send_ptr_button(cast[uint32](ptr), serial, evt_time, code, st)
        b = b + 1
    w_btn[i] = mask


# `at` is where this line STARTS, and it matters: wsysd writes a burst of
# pointer lines and one read picks up several, so a handler that always parsed
# from offset 0 re-read the first line for every line in the buffer. The
# visible symptom was that button presses never arrived -- a move and a click
# land in the same read, and the click was read as a second copy of the move.
def handle_ptr_line(c: uint64, i: uint64, surf: uint32, at: uint64, n: uint64):
    ptr: int32 = conn_pointer[c]
    if ptr == 0:
        return
    # "<kind> <x> <y> <buttons> <dz>"
    p: uint64 = skip_field(&inbuf[0], at, n)
    x: int32 = parse_int_at(&inbuf[0], p, n)
    p = skip_field(&inbuf[0], p, n)
    y: int32 = parse_int_at(&inbuf[0], p, n)
    p = skip_field(&inbuf[0], p, n)
    btn: int32 = parse_int_at(&inbuf[0], p, n)
    if w_in[i] == 0:
        serial = serial + 1
        send_ptr_enter(cast[uint32](ptr), serial, surf, x, y)
        w_in[i] = 1
    evt_time = evt_time + 4
    send_ptr_motion(cast[uint32](ptr), evt_time, x, y)
    btn_edges(c, i, surf, btn)
    send_ptr_frame(cast[uint32](ptr))


# Read whatever is in one window's rings and translate it. The rings are
# written a whole line at a time by wsysd, so a read almost never splits one;
# a split line is DROPPED rather than carried, because the alternative is a
# per-window carry buffer for an event that is one lost mouse position.
def drain_window(i: uint64):
    c: uint64 = cast[uint64](w_conn[i])
    if conn_inuse[c] == 0:
        return
    surf: uint32 = cast[uint32](w_surf[i])
    fd: int32 = w_ptr[i]
    if fd >= 0:
        got: int64 = sys_read_nb(fd, &inbuf[0], 1000)
        s: uint64 = 0
        k: uint64 = 0
        while k < cast[uint64](got):
            if inbuf[k] == 10:
                if k > s:
                    handle_ptr_line(c, i, surf, s, k)
                s = k + 1
            k = k + 1
    fd = w_keys[i]
    if fd >= 0:
        got = sys_read_nb(fd, &inbuf[0], 1000)
        if got > 0 and w_focus[i] == 0:
            kbd: int32 = conn_keyboard[c]
            if kbd != 0:
                serial = serial + 1
                send_kbd_enter(cast[uint32](kbd), serial, surf)
                w_focus[i] = 1
        s = 0
        k = 0
        while k < cast[uint64](got):
            if inbuf[k] == 10:
                # "d <code>": a press of a cooked ASCII byte. wsysd has no
                # release edge to give us, so each byte is delivered as a
                # press immediately followed by a release.
                if k > s + 1:
                    p: uint64 = skip_field(&inbuf[0], s, k)
                    a: int32 = parse_int_at(&inbuf[0], p, k)
                    key_tap(c, ascii_evdev(a), needs_shift(a))
                s = k + 1
            k = k + 1
    if out_pos > 0:
        flush(conn_fd[c])


# --- the screen -------------------------------------------------------

# Top-level BSS, not frame locals: an Adder frame local array is not a place
# to put a buffer on this line.
scr_wp: Array[1, int64]
scr_hp: Array[1, int64]
scrpath: Array[256, uint8]
scrline: Array[64, uint8]


# HOW BIG IS THE SCREEN? This server answers that question for every Wayland
# client -- wl_output.mode, wl_output.geometry and zxdg_output_v1.logical_size
# are all it -- and, through Xwayland, for every X11 client behind it too. It
# used to answer 1280x800 because that is what the VM happens to be, which is
# the exact bug lib/hamscreen.ad exists to end: a plausible wrong answer, on
# any display that is not 1280x800, for the entire Debian namespace at once.
#
# So it comes from the compositor, which is the process that actually set the
# mode, and there is NO fallback. A Wayland server that does not know how big
# its output is has nothing true to tell a client, and inventing a number here
# would be inventing it for Firefox and for Steam simultaneously.
def resolve_screen() -> int32:
    if hamscreen_dims_or_fail(&scr_wp[0], &scr_hp[0],
                              cast[Ptr[uint8]]("wsyswl")) == 0:
        return 0
    scr_w = cast[int32](scr_wp[0])
    scr_h = cast[int32](scr_hp[0])
    # "Maximised" is the screen minus what wsysd's title bar and frame take,
    # not the screen: a client handed the whole thing loses its own chrome off
    # the bottom edge. Small screens get a floor rather than a negative size.
    MAX_W = scr_w - MAX_INSET_W
    MAX_H = scr_h - MAX_INSET_H
    if MAX_W < 320:
        MAX_W = 320
    if MAX_H < 240:
        MAX_H = 240
    return 1


# PUBLISH IT NEXT TO THE SOCKET, because a program on the other side of the
# namespace boundary cannot read /dev/wsys.
#
# Xwayland is started INSIDE the Debian namespace, whose root is the Debian
# tree, so /dev/wsys does not exist for it and lib/hamscreen is unreachable.
# The one name it already shares with us is the directory holding the Wayland
# socket (docs/steam_namespace.md §6). A sibling file there is therefore the
# only fact we can hand across, and it is exactly the Plan 9 answer: publish
# the geometry as a file, and let the client name it.
#
# It matters because a ROOTFUL Xwayland does not size itself from the
# wl_output. Bookworm's 22.1.9 happens to; 24.1.6 does not and comes up
# 640x480 -- measured, tests/linux/x11_geom_probe.sh -- so an X11 session's
# screen size silently depends on the Xwayland version unless it is told.
def publish_screen():
    i: uint64 = 0
    cut: uint64 = 0
    while sockpath[i] != 0:
        if sockpath[i] == 47:                  # '/'
            cut = i
        i = i + 1
    if cut == 0:
        return
    j: uint64 = 0
    while j < cut and j + 16 < 256:
        scrpath[j] = sockpath[j]
        j = j + 1
    str_copy(&scrpath[j], "/hamnix-screen", 256 - j)
    fd: int32 = sys_open_write(cast[Ptr[char]](&scrpath[0]))
    if fd < 0:
        return
    n: uint64 = emit_int(&scrline[0], 0, scr_w)
    scrline[n] = 32
    n = emit_int(&scrline[0], n + 1, scr_h)
    scrline[n] = 10
    sys_write(fd, &scrline[0], n + 1)
    sys_close(fd)


# AND PUBLISH WHAT THIS SERVER IS DOING WITH THE FRAMES IT IS GIVEN, in the
# same directory and for the same reason: it is the one name a program on
# either side of the namespace boundary can open. `cat /run/wayland-0`'s
# sibling `wsyswl-state` from a Debian shell, or from the Hamnix side at
# /n/distro/run/wsyswl-state, and the compositor tells you how many buffers it
# has put on the screen and how many it has thrown away, by reason.
def build_statepath():
    i: uint64 = 0
    cut: uint64 = 0
    while sockpath[i] != 0:
        if sockpath[i] == 47:                  # '/'
            cut = i
        i = i + 1
    if cut == 0:
        return
    j: uint64 = 0
    while j < cut and j + 24 < 256:
        statepath[j] = sockpath[j]
        j = j + 1
    str_copy(&statepath[j], "/wsyswl-state", 256 - j)


def publish_state():
    if statepath[0] == 0:
        return
    if stat_dirty == 0:
        return
    fd: int32 = sys_open_write(cast[Ptr[char]](&statepath[0]))
    if fd < 0:
        return
    st_kv(fd, "commits", S_COMMIT)
    st_kv(fd, "rows", S_ROWS)
    st_kv(fd, "drop_not_a_buffer", S_NOTBUF)
    st_kv(fd, "drop_zero_size", S_BADDIM)
    st_kv(fd, "drop_no_mapping", S_NOMAP)
    st_kv(fd, "drop_unmapped", S_NOBASE)
    st_kv(fd, "drop_no_role", S_NOROLE)
    st_kv(fd, "drop_no_window", S_NOWIN)
    st_kv(fd, "drop_no_slot", S_NOSLOT)
    st_kv(fd, "drop_unconfigured", S_UNCONF)
    st_kv(fd, "drop_row_past_mapping", S_SHORT)
    st_kv(fd, "map_alloc_failed", S_MAPFULL)
    st_kv(fd, "obj_id_refused", S_IDHIGH)
    st_kv(fd, "frame_callbacks_full", S_FCFULL)
    st_kv(fd, "maps_in_use", S_MAPUSE)
    st_kv(fd, "maps_high_water", S_MAPHI)
    st_kv(fd, "max_object_id", S_IDHI)
    st_kv(fd, "windows_high_water", S_WINHI)
    puts(fd, "limits MAXMAP=")
    putd(fd, cast[int32](MAXMAP))
    puts(fd, " MAXOBJ=")
    putd(fd, cast[int32](MAXOBJ))
    puts(fd, " MAXWIN=")
    putd(fd, cast[int32](MAXWIN))
    puts(fd, " FCMAX=")
    putd(fd, cast[int32](FCMAX))
    puts(fd, "\n")
    sys_close(fd)
    stat_dirty = 0


# --- main -------------------------------------------------------------

def main(argc: int32, argv: Ptr[uint64]) -> int32:
    n: int64 = sys_getenv(cast[Ptr[char]]("XDG_RUNTIME_DIR"), &envbuf[0], 256)
    if n > 0:
        k: uint64 = str_copy(&sockpath[0], &envbuf[0], 256)
        str_copy(&sockpath[k], "/wayland-0", 256 - k)
    else:
        str_copy(&sockpath[0], "/tmp/wayland-0", 256)
    if argc > 1:
        str_copy(&sockpath[0], cast[Ptr[uint8]](argv[1]), 256)
    if sys_getenv(cast[Ptr[char]]("WSYSWL_VERBOSE"), &envbuf[0], 256) >= 0:
        verbose = 1

    # BEFORE the socket exists. A client that connects and binds wl_output in
    # the same breath must not be able to catch a zero.
    if resolve_screen() == 0:
        return 1
    puts(1, "wsyswl: screen ")
    putd(1, scr_w)
    puts(1, "x")
    putd(1, scr_h)
    puts(1, "\n")

    i: uint64 = 0
    while i < MAXCONN:
        conn_inuse[i] = 0
        conn_fd[i] = -1
        i = i + 1
    i = 0
    while i < FCMAX:
        fc_conn[i] = -1                 # 0 is a real connection number
        i = i + 1

    listen_fd = sys_unix_listen(cast[Ptr[char]](&sockpath[0]), 8)
    if listen_fd < 0:
        puts(2, "wsyswl: cannot listen on ")
        puts(2, &sockpath[0])
        puts(2, "\n")
        return 1
    publish_screen()
    build_statepath()
    publish_state()
    puts(1, "wsyswl: state file ")
    puts(1, &statepath[0])
    puts(1, "\n")
    puts(1, "wsyswl: listening on ")
    puts(1, &sockpath[0])
    puts(1, "\n")
    puts(1, "wsyswl: point a client at it with WAYLAND_DISPLAY=wayland-0\n")

    while 1 == 1:
        nfd: uint64 = 0
        waitset[0] = listen_fd
        nfd = 1
        i = 0
        while i < MAXCONN:
            if conn_inuse[i] != 0:
                waitset[nfd] = conn_fd[i]
                nfd = nfd + 1
            i = i + 1
        # A short timeout rather than a pure wait: the input rings are files,
        # not descriptors that poll can speak for, so they have to be looked
        # at on a clock. 16 ms is one frame.
        sys_waitfds(&waitset[0], nfd, 16)
        conn_accept()
        i = 0
        while i < MAXCONN:
            if conn_inuse[i] != 0:
                if conn_pump(i) < 0:
                    conn_free(i)
            i = i + 1
        i = 0
        while i < n_win:
            drain_window(i)
            i = i + 1
        # Twice a second, not every 16 ms frame: this is a file rewritten in
        # full and the point of it was to be cheaper than a log.
        tick = tick + 1
        if tick >= 32:
            tick = 0
            publish_state()
    return 0
